ForgeData.register("gcse-science",{"GCSE-SCI-BIO-1":{"label":"Biology 1 — Cells, Genetics, Selection & Disease","color":"#166534","questions":[{"id":"SCI-BIO1-01","spec":"1SC0 Topic 1","stem":"Which feature is present in both prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?","options":{"A":"A cell membrane","B":"A nucleus","C":"Releasing energy","D":"Chloroplasts"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-CELL","scaffold":"Both cell types have a cell membrane, cytoplasm, ribosomes and genetic material. Prokaryotes do not have a nucleus or membrane-bound organelles.","reforge":{"stem":"Which structure controls movement into and out of a cell?","options":{"A":"Controlling osmosis","B":"Cytoplasm","C":"Cell membrane","D":"Ribosome"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-02","spec":"1SC0 Topic 1","stem":"A microscope has a 10× eyepiece and a 40× objective lens. What is its total magnification?","options":{"A":"4×","B":"400×","C":"40×","D":"4,000×"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-MAGNIFICATION","scaffold":"Total magnification is calculated by multiplying the eyepiece magnification by the objective magnification: 10 × 40 = 400×.","reforge":{"stem":"An image is 30 mm long at 150× magnification. What is its actual length?","options":{"A":"180 mm","B":"0.02 mm","C":"4.5 mm","D":"0.2 mm"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-03","spec":"1SC0 Topic 2","stem":"What is the main role of ribosomes in a cell?","options":{"A":"Releasing energy","B":"Controlling osmosis","C":"Making proteins","D":"Storing genetic material"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-RIBO","scaffold":"Ribosomes are the sites of protein synthesis. Mitochondria release energy, while the nucleus contains most of the genetic material.","reforge":{"stem":"Which structure is the main site of aerobic respiration?","options":{"A":"Mitochondrion","B":"Ribosome","C":"Nucleus","D":"The cells combine after division"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-04","spec":"1SC0 Topic 2","stem":"Why does mitosis produce two genetically identical daughter cells?","options":{"A":"The cells combine after division","B":"It makes every fish produce more oxygen","C":"The chromosome number is halved","D":"DNA is copied once before one division"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-MITOSIS","scaffold":"Before mitosis, DNA is replicated. The copied chromosomes are then separated so each daughter cell receives an identical set.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one function of mitosis in a multicellular organism?","options":{"A":"Producing gametes","B":"Growth and tissue repair","C":"Creating genetic variation","D":"Reducing chromosome number"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-05","spec":"1SC0 Topic 3","stem":"What is meant by the term allele?","options":{"A":"A version of a gene","B":"100%","C":"A complete chromosome set","D":"A visible characteristic"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-GENETICS","scaffold":"An allele is an alternative form of a gene. Different alleles can contribute to variation in an inherited characteristic.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the term for an organism's observable characteristic?","options":{"A":"Allele","B":"Two identical dominant alleles","C":"Phenotype","D":"Gamete"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-06","spec":"1SC0 Topic 3","stem":"Two heterozygous parents have a child. What is the probability that the child inherits two recessive alleles?","options":{"A":"0%","B":"25%","C":"Each cell receives different genes","D":"75%"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-GENETICS","scaffold":"A cross of two heterozygous parents gives four equally likely allele combinations. One combination contains two recessive alleles, so the probability is 1/4 or 25%.","reforge":{"stem":"For two heterozygous parents, what is the probability of a dominant phenotype when the dominant allele is fully expressed?","options":{"A":"25%","B":"Two identical dominant alleles","C":"100%","D":"75%"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-07","spec":"1SC0 Topic 4","stem":"Why can a mutation sometimes produce a new characteristic?","options":{"A":"It always improves survival","B":"Identical offspring in every generation","C":"It changes the DNA base sequence","D":"It prevents all reproduction"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-EVOLUTION","scaffold":"A mutation changes the DNA base sequence. This may alter the protein made and therefore affect a characteristic, although many mutations have no observable effect.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the most accurate view of mutations?","options":{"A":"Genetic variation","B":"Mutations only occur in gametes","C":"All mutations are harmful","D":"Mutations are caused only by exercise"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-08","spec":"1SC0 Topic 4","stem":"How does natural selection change the frequency of a useful allele in a population?","options":{"A":"It always improves survival","B":"All mutations are harmful","C":"The allele appears because the population tries","D":"More offspring from carriers"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-EVOLUTION","scaffold":"Variation means some individuals are better adapted. They are more likely to survive and reproduce, so their alleles become more common over generations.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a key condition for natural selection?","options":{"A":"Identical offspring in every generation","B":"Inherited variation between individuals","C":"No competition for resources","D":"Traits acquired during exercise"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-09","spec":"1SC0 Topic 5","stem":"Why are antibiotics ineffective against viruses?","options":{"A":"No bacterial drug target","B":"Viruses are too large to enter","C":"Viruses have thicker cell walls","D":"Viruses can digest antibiotics"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-DISEASE","scaffold":"Antibiotics target bacterial structures or processes. Viruses reproduce inside host cells and do not have the same cellular targets.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one reason new antibiotic-resistant strains can spread quickly?","options":{"A":"All bacteria become viruses","B":"Antibiotics create useful organs","C":"Resistance can be inherited by bacteria","D":"Resistance stops bacterial reproduction"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-10","spec":"1SC0 Topic 5","stem":"What is the purpose of a control group in a drug trial?","options":{"A":"To receive the largest dose","B":"To provide a comparison","C":"To guarantee a positive result","D":"To remove all variables"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-TRIAL","scaffold":"A control group provides a comparison with the treatment group. A placebo can help reduce the effects of expectation when appropriate.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are double-blind trials useful?","options":{"A":"They eliminate every risk","B":"They make a placebo active","C":"They prevent data collection","D":"Less trial bias"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-11","spec":"1SC0 Topic 5","stem":"What is vaccination intended to produce?","options":{"A":"More pathogenic microbes","B":"A permanent rise in body temperature","C":"Memory cells that respond rapidly","D":"Immediate destruction of every pathogen"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-IMMUNITY","scaffold":"Vaccination exposes the immune system to harmless antigens. Memory cells can then trigger a faster, stronger response if the pathogen is encountered later.","reforge":{"stem":"Why may a booster vaccination be needed?","options":{"A":"Renew immune memory","B":"To remove all white blood cells","C":"To make the pathogen reproduce","D":"To prevent antibody production"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-12","spec":"1SC0 Topic 5","stem":"Why can monoclonal antibodies be useful in medicine?","options":{"A":"They increase all hormone levels","B":"They replace every enzyme","C":"They work only on healthy cells","D":"They bind to one specific target"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-MONOCLONAL","scaffold":"Monoclonal antibodies are identical antibodies made from a single clone of cells. Their specificity allows them to bind to a particular antigen or cell marker.","reforge":{"stem":"What feature makes a monoclonal antibody specific?","options":{"A":"It has no protein structure","B":"Specific antigen binding","C":"It destroys every nearby cell","D":"It changes into a red blood cell"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-13","spec":"1SC0 Topic 2","stem":"Why do cells become specialised during development?","options":{"A":"Gene expression","B":"They lose all their DNA","C":"They stop making proteins","D":"They become identical to gametes"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-DIFFERENTIATION","scaffold":"Most body cells contain the same genetic information, but different genes are expressed in different cells. This produces specialised structures and functions.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a feature of a specialised cell?","options":{"A":"It cannot contain proteins","B":"It has no cell membrane","C":"Specialised function","D":"It always divides by meiosis"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-14","spec":"1SC0 Topic 2","stem":"What is the main feature of active transport?","options":{"A":"It uses no energy","B":"Against the gradient","C":"It only moves water","D":"It happens through the cell wall"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-DIFFUSION","scaffold":"Active transport uses energy from respiration to move substances from a lower concentration to a higher concentration, against the concentration gradient.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do root hair cells need active transport?","options":{"A":"Mineral ions are always gases","B":"Water cannot cross membranes","C":"Roots contain no mitochondria","D":"Low soil ion concentration"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-15","spec":"1SC0 Topic 2","stem":"What happens to an enzyme's active site when it is denatured?","options":{"A":"It gains a nucleus","B":"It becomes a substrate","C":"Shape changes","D":"It produces more heat"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-ENZYME","scaffold":"High temperature or extreme pH can disrupt bonds that hold an enzyme's shape. The active site changes so the substrate no longer fits effectively.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does an enzyme-controlled reaction slow at a very low temperature?","options":{"A":"Low particle energy","B":"The enzyme is always denatured","C":"A lower substrate concentration","D":"The active site disappears permanently"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-16","spec":"1SC0 Topic 2","stem":"Which reagent is used to test a food sample for starch?","options":{"A":"Ethanol","B":"Biuret solution","C":"Benedict's solution","D":"Iodine solution"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-FOOD","scaffold":"Iodine solution turns blue-black when starch is present. Biuret tests protein and Benedict's solution can test reducing sugars after heating.","reforge":{"stem":"What colour indicates protein in a positive Biuret test?","options":{"A":"Blue-black","B":"Lilac","C":"Brick-red","D":"Orange"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-17","spec":"1SC0 Topic 3","stem":"Which base pairs with adenine in a DNA molecule?","options":{"A":"Thymine","B":"Cytosine","C":"Uracil","D":"Another adenine"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-DNA","scaffold":"In DNA, adenine pairs with thymine and cytosine pairs with guanine. Complementary pairing helps DNA copy accurately.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the role of DNA in a cell?","options":{"A":"It dissolves cell walls","B":"It releases all cell energy","C":"Genetic instructions","D":"It prevents protein formation"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-18","spec":"1SC0 Topic 4","stem":"What is the purpose of a vector in genetic modification?","options":{"A":"It destroys every chromosome","B":"Carries a desired gene","C":"It prevents DNA replication","D":"It removes all variation"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-VECTOR","scaffold":"A vector, such as a plasmid or virus, can carry a desired gene into a target cell. The gene may then be expressed by that cell.","reforge":{"stem":"Which enzyme is used to join a desired gene into a vector?","options":{"A":"Catalase","B":"Amylase","C":"Lipase","D":"Ligase"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-19","spec":"1SC0 Topic 4","stem":"What is a possible disadvantage of selective breeding?","options":{"A":"It prevents inherited features","B":"It always creates new species","C":"Reduced variation","D":"It removes all useful traits"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-BREEDING","scaffold":"Breeding from a small group with desired characteristics can reduce the gene pool. This may increase the risk that inherited disorders become common.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might selective breeding make a population vulnerable to disease?","options":{"A":"Shared alleles","B":"Pathogens cannot enter populations","C":"All individuals become unrelated","D":"Breeding stops reproduction"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-20","spec":"1SC0 Topic 5","stem":"How can a respiratory pathogen spread between people?","options":{"A":"Through photosynthesis","B":"Only through inherited DNA","C":"By turning into a mineral ion","D":"Cough droplets"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-DISEASE","scaffold":"Respiratory pathogens can spread in droplets or aerosols. Hand hygiene, ventilation and limiting close contact can reduce transmission.","reforge":{"stem":"Which action can reduce transmission of a pathogen on surfaces?","options":{"A":"Sharing used tissues","B":"Surface cleaning","C":"Reducing ventilation","D":"Ignoring symptoms"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-21","spec":"1SC0 Topic 5","stem":"Why are antibodies specific to particular pathogens?","options":{"A":"Matching antigen sites","B":"They contain no proteins","C":"They attack every cell equally","D":"They are made by red blood cells"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-DEFENCE","scaffold":"An antibody has a complementary binding site for a particular antigen. This allows it to bind to the pathogen or toxin and help target it for destruction.","reforge":{"stem":"What do white blood cells produce after recognising a foreign antigen?","options":{"A":"Starch granules","B":"More red blood cells only","C":"Specific antibodies","D":"Bile salts"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-22","spec":"1SC0 Topic 5","stem":"Why is a placebo used in some clinical trials?","options":{"A":"It contains the highest dose","B":"Treatment comparison","C":"It guarantees the drug works","D":"It replaces ethical approval"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-TRIAL","scaffold":"A placebo looks like the treatment but lacks its active ingredient. Comparing responses helps identify effects caused by the drug rather than expectation.","reforge":{"stem":"Why should a clinical trial use a large sample where possible?","options":{"A":"It prevents all variation","B":"It removes the need for controls","C":"It guarantees no side effects","D":"Representative results"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-23","spec":"1SC0 Topic 4","stem":"What can fossils provide evidence for?","options":{"A":"The absence of mutation","B":"The exact colour of every extinct organism","C":"Evolution and ancestry","D":"A complete record of all life"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-EVOLUTION","scaffold":"Fossils can show how organisms changed over time and may reveal similarities between species. The fossil record is incomplete, so conclusions have limits.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the fossil record incomplete?","options":{"A":"Decay without fossils","B":"All fossils dissolve instantly","C":"Only living animals can fossilise","D":"Scientists cannot compare fossils"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"SCI-BIO1-24","spec":"1SC0 Topic 4","stem":"How can a rapid environmental change cause a species to become extinct?","options":{"A":"All competitors disappear","B":"Every individual gains a useful mutation","C":"The environment stops selecting","D":"Adaptation cannot keep up"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-EVOLUTION","scaffold":"If conditions change faster than a population can adapt, survival and reproduction may fall. The population can decline to extinction.","reforge":{"stem":"Which factor can increase extinction risk?","options":{"A":"Greater genetic variation","B":"Habitat loss","C":"A stable food supply","D":"Successful conservation"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-001","stem":"Which feature is present in both prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells? (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"A cell membrane","B":"A nucleus","C":"Releasing energy","D":"Chloroplasts"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-CELL","scaffold":"Both cell types have a cell membrane, cytoplasm, ribosomes and genetic material. Prokaryotes do not have a nucleus or membrane-bound organelles.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which structure controls movement into and out of a cell?","options":{"A":"Releasing energy","B":"Cytoplasm","C":"Cell membrane","D":"Ribosome"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-007","stem":"Why can a mutation sometimes produce a new characteristic? (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"It always improves survival","B":"It changes the DNA base sequence","C":"Identical offspring in every generation","D":"It prevents all reproduction"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-EVOLUTION","scaffold":"A mutation changes the DNA base sequence. This may alter the protein made and therefore affect a characteristic, although many mutations have no observable effect.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which option about mutations is most accurate?","options":{"A":"Mutations are caused only by exercise","B":"Mutations only occur in gametes","C":"All mutations are harmful","D":"Genetic variation"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-013","stem":"Why do cells become specialised during development? (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"They stop making proteins","B":"They lose all their DNA","C":"Gene expression","D":"They become identical to gametes"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-DIFFERENTIATION","scaffold":"Most body cells contain the same genetic information, but different genes are expressed in different cells. This produces specialised structures and functions.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What is a feature of a specialised cell?","options":{"A":"Specialised function","B":"It has no cell membrane","C":"It cannot contain proteins","D":"It always divides by meiosis"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-019","stem":"What is a possible disadvantage of selective breeding? (second formulation)","options":{"A":"It prevents inherited features","B":"It always creates new species","C":"It removes all useful traits","D":"Reduced variation"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-BREEDING","scaffold":"Breeding from a small group with desired characteristics can reduce the gene pool. This may increase the risk that inherited disorders become common.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Why might selective breeding make a population vulnerable to disease?","options":{"A":"Pathogens cannot enter populations","B":"Shared alleles","C":"All individuals become unrelated","D":"Breeding stops reproduction"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-025","stem":"What does the atomic number of an element show?","options":{"A":"The number of protons","B":"Neutrons minus electrons","C":"The total mass of the atom","D":"The number of shells"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ATOM","scaffold":"The atomic number equals the number of protons in the nucleus. In a neutral atom it also equals the number of electrons.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What is the mass number of an atom?","options":{"A":"Electrons in the outer shell","B":"Protons plus electrons","C":"Protons plus neutrons","D":"The number of protons"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-031","stem":"What is the best way to prepare a pure, dry soluble salt from an acid and an insoluble base?","options":{"A":"Salt, water and carbon dioxide","B":"Add excess base, filter, then crystallise","C":"Trees create carbon dioxide by absorbing light","D":"Filter the acid before mixing"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ACIDMETAL","scaffold":"Adding excess insoluble base ensures all acid reacts. The excess is filtered off, then the filtrate is concentrated and cooled to form crystals.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Why is excess insoluble base removed by filtration?","options":{"A":"Metal and carbon","B":"Water and oxygen","C":"It neutralises the salt","D":"It did not dissolve"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-037","stem":"why do ionic compounds conduct electricity when molten but not when solid?","options":{"A":"their molecules become larger","B":"The atoms disappear when solid","C":"Mobile ions","D":"The solid contains no charge"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-BOND","scaffold":"In a solid ionic lattice, ions are fixed in position. When molten, the ions can move and carry charge through the liquid.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What holds oppositely charged ions together in an ionic lattice?","options":{"A":"Ionic attraction","B":"Shared electron pairs","C":"Magnetic repulsion","D":"Gravity alone"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-043","stem":"What does oxidation mean in terms of oxygen?","options":{"A":"Gain of electrons only","B":"Loss of oxygen","C":"Loss of protons","D":"Gain oxygen"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-REDOX","scaffold":"Oxidation can be described as gain of oxygen, while reduction is loss of oxygen. The terms can also refer to electron transfer.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"In a metal oxide reduced by carbon, what happens to the metal oxide?","options":{"A":"It gains oxygen","B":"Oxygen loss","C":"It becomes an acid","D":"It forms water only"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-049","stem":"A cyclist travels 150 m in 12 s. What is the average speed?","options":{"A":"12.5 m/s","B":"14 m/s²","C":"300 m","D":"1,800 m/s"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-MOTION","scaffold":"Average speed = distance ÷ time = 150 ÷ 12 = 12.5 m/s. Always check that the units match the equation.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A car travels at 20 m/s for 15 s. What distance does it cover?","options":{"A":"The object is moving fastest","B":"35 m","C":"300 m","D":"750 m"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-055","stem":"A 2 kg object is lifted 5 m. Using g = 10 N/kg, what is its gravitational potential energy increase?","options":{"A":"It halves","B":"More height","C":"Less mass","D":"Lower height"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-ENERGY","scaffold":"Gravitational potential energy change = mass × gravitational field strength × height = 2 × 10 × 5 = 100 J.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which change increases the gravitational potential energy gained when lifting an object?","options":{"A":"The force becomes mass","B":"A smaller mass","C":"More height","D":"A greater height"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"GCSE-PHASE6-BIO1-01","stem":"Which factor can denature an enzyme by changing the shape of its active site?","options":{"A":"A shorter diffusion distance","B":"A lower substrate concentration","C":"A temperature above its optimum","D":"A smaller volume of water"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-ENZYME","scaffold":"High temperature can disrupt bonds maintaining the enzyme's tertiary structure.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies the idea to this case: Which factor can denature an enzyme by changing the shape of its active site?","options":{"A":"A temperature above its optimum","B":"The active site disappears permanently","C":"It produces more heat","D":"It becomes a substrate"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"},{"id":"GCSE-PHASE6-BIO1-02","stem":"A heterozygous individual has which pair of alleles?","options":{"A":"Two identical dominant alleles","B":"No alleles for that gene","C":"Each cell receives different genes","D":"Two different alleles for a gene"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-GENETICS","scaffold":"Heterozygous means the two alleles at a gene locus are different.","reforge":{"stem":"In a practical example, which answer fits? A heterozygous individual has which pair of alleles?","options":{"A":"Mutations are caused only by exercise","B":"Two different alleles for a gene","C":"Allele","D":"A visible characteristic"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-1"}]},"GCSE-SCI-CHEM-1":{"label":"Chemistry 1 — Matter, Reactions, Metals & Equilibria","color":"#9a3412","questions":[{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-01","spec":"1SC0 Topic 1","stem":"What does the atomic number of an element show?","options":{"A":"The number of protons","B":"The number of neutrons","C":"The total mass of the atom","D":"The number of shells"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ATOM","scaffold":"The atomic number equals the number of protons in the nucleus. In a neutral atom it also equals the number of electrons.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the mass number of an atom?","options":{"A":"Electrons in the outer shell","B":"Protons plus electrons","C":"Protons plus neutrons","D":"Neutrons minus electrons"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-02","spec":"1SC0 Topic 1","stem":"Why do isotopes of an element have similar chemical properties?","options":{"A":"They have no electrons","B":"They have the same number of protons","C":"Aluminium is less reactive than carbon","D":"They are all radioactive"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ISOTOPE","scaffold":"Isotopes have the same electron arrangement because they have the same atomic number. Chemical reactions depend mainly on electrons.","reforge":{"stem":"How do isotopes of one element differ?","options":{"A":"They have different chemical identities","B":"They have different proton numbers","C":"They have different element symbols","D":"They have different neutron numbers"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-03","spec":"1SC0 Topic 2","stem":"Which change is a physical change?","options":{"A":"Burning methane","B":"The beaker","C":"Melting ice","D":"Reacting acid with metal"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-STATES","scaffold":"Melting changes the state of water but does not make a new substance. Chemical changes form new substances with different properties.","reforge":{"stem":"During boiling, what happens to the particles?","options":{"A":"Particles spread apart","B":"They become new atoms","C":"They all become gases","D":"They form a new compound"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-04","spec":"1SC0 Topic 2","stem":"Which method separates a soluble solid from a solution?","options":{"A":"Transporting sugars from leaves","B":"Filtration alone","C":"Magnetic separation","D":"Evaporation or crystallisation"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-SEPARATION","scaffold":"Evaporation removes solvent and crystallisation can obtain the dissolved solid. Filtration separates an insoluble solid from a liquid.","reforge":{"stem":"Which technique separates different dyes in an ink sample?","options":{"A":"Distillation only","B":"Chromatography","C":"Titration","D":"Precipitation"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-05","spec":"1SC0 Topic 3","stem":"What is the result when an acid reacts with a metal carbonate?","options":{"A":"Salt, water and carbon dioxide","B":"They have different element symbols","C":"Water and oxygen","D":"Metal and carbon"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ACIDMETAL","scaffold":"Acid plus metal carbonate produces a salt, water and carbon dioxide. The gas can be identified because it turns limewater cloudy.","reforge":{"stem":"What gas is usually formed when an acid reacts with a metal?","options":{"A":"The solution becomes colourless","B":"Carbon dioxide","C":"Hydrogen","D":"Chlorine"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-06","spec":"1SC0 Topic 3","stem":"Why does a strong acid have a lower pH than a weak acid of the same concentration?","options":{"A":"It contains more water","B":"It ionises more completely","C":"It has no hydrogen ions","D":"It is always more concentrated"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ACID","scaffold":"A strong acid ionises almost completely in water, producing a higher concentration of hydrogen ions and therefore a lower pH.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a pH decrease from 4 to 3 represent?","options":{"A":"A neutral solution","B":"One tenth as many hydrogen ions","C":"No change in acidity","D":"Ten times more hydrogen ions"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-07","spec":"1SC0 Topic 3","stem":"What is the best way to prepare a pure, dry soluble salt from an acid and an insoluble base?","options":{"A":"Salt, water and carbon dioxide","B":"Trees create carbon dioxide by absorbing light","C":"Add excess base, filter, then crystallise","D":"Filter the acid before mixing"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ACIDMETAL","scaffold":"Adding excess insoluble base ensures all acid reacts. The excess is filtered off, then the filtrate is concentrated and cooled to form crystals.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is excess insoluble base removed by filtration?","options":{"A":"It did not dissolve","B":"It is a gas","C":"It neutralises the salt","D":"Water and oxygen"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-08","spec":"1SC0 Topic 3","stem":"In electrolysis of molten lead bromide, which particles carry charge through the liquid?","options":{"A":"remove all ions from solution","B":"Protons only","C":"Electrons only","D":"Mobile ions"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ELECTRO","scaffold":"Molten ionic compounds contain mobile positive and negative ions. These ions move to opposite electrodes and carry charge through the electrolyte.","reforge":{"stem":"Which ion is discharged at the cathode during electrolysis?","options":{"A":"A neutral molecule","B":"A positive ion","C":"A negative ion","D":"A proton from the wire"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-09","spec":"1SC0 Topic 4","stem":"Why can carbon be used to extract copper from copper oxide?","options":{"A":"Carbon is more reactive than copper","B":"Copper is more reactive than carbon","C":"Carbon oxide is a metal","D":"Copper cannot form ions"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-EXTRACT","scaffold":"Carbon is above copper in the reactivity series, so it can remove oxygen from copper oxide in a reduction reaction.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main raw material for extracting iron in a blast furnace?","options":{"A":"No carbon dioxide is produced anywhere","B":"Pure iron metal","C":"Iron oxide ore","D":"Copper carbonate"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-10","spec":"1SC0 Topic 4","stem":"Why is electrolysis used to extract aluminium rather than carbon reduction?","options":{"A":"Direct current changes direction constantly","B":"Aluminium is more reactive than carbon","C":"Aluminium has no melting point","D":"Carbon cannot conduct electricity"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-EXTRACT","scaffold":"Aluminium is more reactive than carbon, so its oxide is not reduced by carbon. Electrolysis is needed, although it uses considerable energy.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one environmental cost of extracting aluminium by electrolysis?","options":{"A":"The metal cannot be recycled","B":"No carbon dioxide is produced anywhere","C":"The ore becomes radioactive","D":"High electricity demand"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-11","spec":"1SC0 Topic 4","stem":"What happens to the position of equilibrium when more reactant is added?","options":{"A":"High temperature always gives the highest yield","B":"It always stops","C":"It shifts to make more products","D":"It removes the catalyst"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-EQUIL","scaffold":"Le Chatelier's principle predicts that equilibrium shifts in the direction that reduces the change. Adding a reactant favours the forward reaction.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a catalyst do to a reversible reaction at equilibrium?","options":{"A":"Speeds both directions equally","B":"They have different element symbols","C":"Removes one product","D":"Temperature has no effect"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-12","spec":"1SC0 Topic 4","stem":"Why may industry use a compromise temperature for an exothermic equilibrium reaction?","options":{"A":"High temperature always gives the highest yield","B":"Low temperature gives no products","C":"Temperature has no effect","D":"Faster rate, lower yield"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-EQUIL","scaffold":"For an exothermic forward reaction, lower temperature favours products but slows the rate. Industry chooses a compromise between yield and production speed.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the effect of increasing pressure on an equilibrium with fewer gas molecules on the product side?","options":{"A":"It always stops","B":"Fewer gas molecules","C":"Changes the equilibrium yield","D":"It destroys the catalyst"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-13","spec":"1SC0 Topic 1","stem":"Why do ionic compounds conduct electricity when molten but not when solid?","options":{"A":"Mobile ions","B":"The atoms disappear when solid","C":"their molecules become larger","D":"The solid contains no charge"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-BOND","scaffold":"In a solid ionic lattice, ions are fixed in position. When molten, the ions can move and carry charge through the liquid.","reforge":{"stem":"What holds oppositely charged ions together in an ionic lattice?","options":{"A":"Magnetic repulsion","B":"Shared electron pairs","C":"Ionic attraction","D":"Gravity alone"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-14","spec":"1SC0 Topic 1","stem":"Why must a chemical equation be balanced?","options":{"A":"Products need more atoms","B":"Atoms conserved","C":"Mass is created","D":"Charges are ignored"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-MASS","scaffold":"Balancing ensures the same number of each type of atom appears on both sides. Chemical reactions rearrange atoms rather than create or destroy them.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a state symbol (aq) indicate?","options":{"A":"A pure liquid fuel","B":"A solid metal","C":"A gas only","D":"Dissolved in water"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-15","spec":"1SC0 Topic 1","stem":"What is the empirical formula of a compound with a 1:2 ratio of carbon to oxygen atoms?","options":{"A":"CO","B":"C2O","C":"CO2","D":"C2O2"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-FORMULA","scaffold":"The empirical formula gives the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms. A carbon-to-oxygen ratio of 1:2 is written CO2.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a molecular formula show?","options":{"A":"Actual atom numbers","B":"Only the simplest ratio","C":"The reaction rate","D":"The melting point"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-16","spec":"1SC0 Topic 2","stem":"What happens to the particles of a solute when it dissolves?","options":{"A":"They all become gases","B":"They become new elements","C":"They stop existing","D":"Particles spread out"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-STATES","scaffold":"Dissolving separates solute particles and disperses them among solvent particles. No new substance is made by this physical process.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the solvent in a salt solution made with water?","options":{"A":"Salt","B":"Water","C":"The beaker","D":"The air above it"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-17","spec":"1SC0 Topic 3","stem":"Why is a burette useful in an acid–alkali titration?","options":{"A":"Measured variable volume","B":"It removes all water","C":"It measures temperature only","D":"It identifies every ion"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-TITRATION","scaffold":"A burette allows a solution to be added accurately while its volume is read. This helps determine the volume needed for neutralisation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the purpose of an indicator in a titration?","options":{"A":"It filters the salt","B":"It increases the acid concentration","C":"Colour-change end point","D":"It prevents neutralisation"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-18","spec":"1SC0 Topic 3","stem":"What happens to positive ions at the cathode during electrolysis?","options":{"A":"They lose neutrons","B":"Gain electrons","C":"They form neutral gases only","D":"They move through the wire"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ELECTRO","scaffold":"Positive ions are attracted to the negative cathode. They gain electrons in reduction reactions at the electrode.","reforge":{"stem":"What happens to negative ions at the anode?","options":{"A":"They stop moving","B":"They gain protons","C":"They become the power supply","D":"Lose electrons"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-19","spec":"1SC0 Topic 3","stem":"What does oxidation mean in terms of oxygen?","options":{"A":"Gain of electrons only","B":"Loss of oxygen","C":"Gain oxygen","D":"Loss of protons"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-REDOX","scaffold":"Oxidation can be described as gain of oxygen, while reduction is loss of oxygen. The terms can also refer to electron transfer.","reforge":{"stem":"In a metal oxide reduced by carbon, what happens to the metal oxide?","options":{"A":"Oxygen loss","B":"It gains oxygen","C":"It becomes an acid","D":"It forms water only"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-20","spec":"1SC0 Topic 3","stem":"Why does painting an iron surface reduce rusting?","options":{"A":"It removes the iron atoms","B":"It increases the iron's reactivity","C":"It supplies more water","D":"Oxygen and water barrier"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-REDOX","scaffold":"Rusting requires both oxygen and water. Paint prevents these reactants reaching the iron, provided the coating remains intact.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can zinc protect iron even when the surface is scratched?","options":{"A":"Zinc prevents every collision","B":"Zinc oxidises first","C":"Iron becomes a noble gas","D":"Zinc removes all water from air"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-21","spec":"1SC0 Topic 4","stem":"What is the effect of increasing pressure on an equilibrium with more gas molecules on the reactant side?","options":{"A":"Fewer gas molecules","B":"It always stops","C":"It has no effect on gases","D":"It stops the forward reaction"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-EQUIL","scaffold":"Increasing pressure favours the side with fewer gas molecules because this reduces the pressure change. The outcome depends on the equation.","reforge":{"stem":"What happens if both sides of a gas equilibrium have the same number of molecules?","options":{"A":"Reactants always form","B":"Products always form","C":"No pressure shift","D":"The catalyst is removed"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-22","spec":"1SC0 Topic 4","stem":"For an endothermic forward reaction, what does increasing temperature generally do to the equilibrium yield of products?","options":{"A":"It always decreases the rate","B":"Higher product yield","C":"It has no effect","D":"It removes reactants"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-EQUIL2","scaffold":"An endothermic forward reaction absorbs heat. Increasing temperature favours the direction that absorbs the added heat, increasing the equilibrium product yield.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the energy transfer in an endothermic reaction?","options":{"A":"No energy changes","B":"Energy leaves to the surroundings","C":"Energy is destroyed","D":"Energy enters"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-23","spec":"1SC0 Topic 3","stem":"A reaction in an open flask appears to lose mass. What is the most likely explanation?","options":{"A":"The balance made mass","B":"Atoms were destroyed","C":"Escaped gas","D":"The reaction stopped conservation"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-MASS","scaffold":"If a gas product escapes from an open flask, the measured mass falls. The total mass is still conserved when the escaped gas is included.","reforge":{"stem":"Why may a solid product gain mass during combustion?","options":{"A":"Oxygen added","B":"Atoms appear from nowhere","C":"The balance creates oxygen","D":"Combustion destroys mass"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM1-24","spec":"1SC0 Topic 2","stem":"What does a single spot on a chromatogram usually suggest about a sample?","options":{"A":"It is necessarily radioactive","B":"It contains every possible dye","C":"It has no solvent","D":"One soluble substance"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-CHROM","scaffold":"A single spot can indicate one soluble component under the conditions used. Comparing positions with known substances can support identification.","reforge":{"stem":"What does the distance travelled by a spot help compare?","options":{"A":"The mass of the paper","B":"Compare Rf values","C":"The room temperature only","D":"The number of protons"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-002","stem":"A microscope has a 10× eyepiece and a 40× objective lens. What is its total magnification?","options":{"A":"400×","B":"4×","C":"40×","D":"4,000×"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-MAGNIFICATION","scaffold":"Total magnification is calculated by multiplying the eyepiece magnification by the objective magnification: 10 × 40 = 400×.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"An image is 30 mm long at 150× magnification. What is its actual length?","options":{"A":"180 mm","B":"0.02 mm","C":"0.2 mm","D":"4.5 mm"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-008","stem":"How does natural selection change the frequency of a useful allele in a population?","options":{"A":"It always improves survival","B":"More offspring from carriers","C":"The allele appears because the population tries","D":"All mutations are harmful"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-EVOLUTION","scaffold":"Variation means some individuals are better adapted. They are more likely to survive and reproduce, so their alleles become more common over generations.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What is a key condition for natural selection?","options":{"A":"Identical offspring in every generation","B":"Traits acquired during exercise","C":"No competition for resources","D":"Inherited variation between individuals"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-014","stem":"What is the main feature of active transport?","options":{"A":"It uses no energy","B":"It only moves water","C":"Against the gradient","D":"It happens through the cell wall"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-DIFFUSION","scaffold":"Active transport uses energy from respiration to move substances from a lower concentration to a higher concentration, against the concentration gradient.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Why do root hair cells need active transport?","options":{"A":"Low soil ion concentration","B":"Water cannot cross membranes","C":"Roots contain no mitochondria","D":"Mineral ions are always gases"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-020","stem":"How can a respiratory pathogen spread between people?","options":{"A":"Through photosynthesis","B":"Only through inherited DNA","C":"By turning into a mineral ion","D":"Cough droplets"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-DISEASE","scaffold":"Respiratory pathogens can spread in droplets or aerosols. Hand hygiene, ventilation and limiting close contact can reduce transmission.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which action can reduce transmission of a pathogen on surfaces?","options":{"A":"Sharing used tissues","B":"Surface cleaning","C":"Reducing ventilation","D":"Ignoring symptoms"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-026","stem":"Why do isotopes of an element have similar chemical properties? (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"They have the same number of protons","B":"They have no electrons","C":"They have different chemical identities","D":"They are all radioactive"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ISOTOPE","scaffold":"Isotopes have the same electron arrangement because they have the same atomic number. Chemical reactions depend mainly on electrons.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"How do isotopes of one element differ?","options":{"A":"They have no electrons","B":"They have different proton numbers","C":"They have different neutron numbers","D":"They have different element symbols"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-032","stem":"In electrolysis of molten lead bromide, which particles carry charge through the liquid? (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"remove all ions from solution","B":"Mobile ions","C":"Electrons only","D":"Protons only"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ELECTRO","scaffold":"Molten ionic compounds contain mobile positive and negative ions. These ions move to opposite electrodes and carry charge through the electrolyte.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which ion is discharged at the cathode during electrolysis?","options":{"A":"A neutral molecule","B":"A proton from the wire","C":"They form neutral gases only","D":"A positive ion"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-038","stem":"why must a chemical equation be balanced? (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"Products need more atoms","B":"Mass is created","C":"Atoms conserved","D":"Charges are ignored"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-MASS","scaffold":"Balancing ensures the same number of each type of atom appears on both sides. Chemical reactions rearrange atoms rather than create or destroy them.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What does a state symbol (aq) indicate?","options":{"A":"Dissolved in water","B":"A solid metal","C":"A gas only","D":"A pure liquid fuel"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-044","stem":"Why does painting an iron surface reduce rusting? (second formulation)","options":{"A":"It removes the iron atoms","B":"It increases the iron's reactivity","C":"It supplies more water","D":"Oxygen and water barrier"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-REDOX","scaffold":"Rusting requires both oxygen and water. Paint prevents these reactants reaching the iron, provided the coating remains intact.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Why can zinc protect iron even when the surface is scratched?","options":{"A":"Zinc prevents every collision","B":"Zinc oxidises first","C":"Iron becomes a noble gas","D":"Zinc removes all water from air"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-050","stem":"What is the difference between a scalar and a vector quantity?","options":{"A":"A vector has magnitude and direction","B":"It supplies extra power during normal use","C":"Only vectors have units","D":"Distance"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-SCALAR-VECTOR","scaffold":"A scalar has magnitude only, while a vector has both magnitude and direction. Speed is scalar; velocity is vector.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which is a vector quantity?","options":{"A":"Distance","B":"Speed","C":"Velocity","D":"Mass"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-056","stem":"Which feature is common to all transverse waves?","options":{"A":"A complete record of all life","B":"Perpendicular vibrations","C":"They require a vacuum","D":"It becomes radioactive"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-WAVE","scaffold":"In a transverse wave, the vibrations are at right angles to the direction of energy transfer. Light and water waves are examples.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What is the frequency of a wave with a period of 0.25 s?","options":{"A":"Its frequency must become zero","B":"Its energy is always destroyed","C":"25 Hz","D":"4 Hz"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"GCSE-PHASE6-CHEM1-01","stem":"Ionic compounds conduct electricity when molten because: ","options":{"A":"their electrons disappear","B":"their atoms become neutral","C":"their ions are free to move","D":"their molecules become larger"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-BOND","scaffold":"Ions are fixed in a solid lattice but mobile when molten or dissolved.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies the idea to this case: Ionic compounds conduct electricity when molten because: ","options":{"A":"their ions are free to move","B":"Gravity alone","C":"Magnetic repulsion","D":"Antibiotics create useful organs"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"},{"id":"GCSE-PHASE6-CHEM1-02","stem":"Electrolysis of an aqueous solution uses electricity to: ","options":{"A":"They form neutral gases only","B":"turn every metal into a gas","C":"Trees create carbon dioxide by absorbing light","D":"decompose ionic substances into products"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ELECTRO","scaffold":"Electrical energy drives a non-spontaneous chemical decomposition.","reforge":{"stem":"In a practical example, which answer fits? Electrolysis of an aqueous solution uses electricity to: ","options":{"A":"A proton from the wire","B":"decompose ionic substances into products","C":"The allele appears because the population tries","D":"They move through the wire"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-1"}]},"GCSE-SCI-PHYS-1":{"label":"Physics 1 — Motion, Energy, Waves, Light & Radioactivity","color":"#1d4ed8","questions":[{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-01","spec":"1SC0 Topic 1","stem":"A cyclist travels 150 m in 12 s. What is the average speed?","options":{"A":"12.5 m/s","B":"14 m/s²","C":"Energy","D":"1,800 m/s"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-MOTION","scaffold":"Average speed = distance ÷ time = 150 ÷ 12 = 12.5 m/s. Always check that the units match the equation.","reforge":{"stem":"A car travels at 20 m/s for 15 s. What distance does it cover?","options":{"A":"1.3 m","B":"35 m","C":"300 m","D":"750 m"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-02","spec":"1SC0 Topic 1","stem":"What is the difference between a scalar and a vector quantity?","options":{"A":"A scalar has direction; a vector does not","B":"A vector has magnitude and direction","C":"Only vectors have units","D":"Scalars cannot be measured"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-SCALAR-VECTOR","scaffold":"A scalar has magnitude only, while a vector has both magnitude and direction. Speed is scalar; velocity is vector.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a vector quantity?","options":{"A":"Distance","B":"Speed","C":"Mass","D":"Velocity"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-03","spec":"1SC0 Topic 2","stem":"What does the gradient of a distance–time graph represent?","options":{"A":"Force","B":"Acceleration","C":"Speed","D":"Energy"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-MOTION","scaffold":"The gradient is change in distance divided by change in time, which is speed. A steeper line represents a greater speed.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a horizontal section on a distance–time graph show?","options":{"A":"The object is stationary","B":"Mass becomes negative","C":"The object has zero mass","D":"The object is moving fastest"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-04","spec":"1SC0 Topic 2","stem":"A resultant force acts on an object. What must happen?","options":{"A":"Its mass changes","B":"Its temperature falls","C":"It becomes stationary","D":"It accelerates"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-NEWTON","scaffold":"A non-zero resultant force causes acceleration. The acceleration may be a change in speed, direction or both.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the resultant force when an object moves at constant velocity?","options":{"A":"7 N","B":"Zero","C":"Greater than its mass","D":"Equal to its weight"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-05","spec":"1SC0 Topic 2","stem":"Which equation links force, mass and acceleration?","options":{"A":"F = m × a","B":"Acceleration increases forever","C":"F = m ÷ a","D":"F = m + a"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-NEWTON","scaffold":"Newton's second law is F = m × a. The force is measured in newtons when mass is in kilograms and acceleration in m/s².","reforge":{"stem":"A 4 kg trolley accelerates at 3 m/s². What is the resultant force?","options":{"A":"7 N","B":"1.3 N","C":"12 N","D":"0.75 N"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-06","spec":"1SC0 Topic 3","stem":"Which option describes conservation of energy?","options":{"A":"Energy is destroyed by friction between the surfaces","B":"Energy is transferred, not created or destroyed","C":"Only useful energy is conserved","D":"Energy is created in batteries"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-ENERGY","scaffold":"The total energy of a closed system remains constant. Transfers may spread energy to the surroundings, often by heating.","reforge":{"stem":"What happens to energy dissipated by friction?","options":{"A":"It disappears","B":"It stops existing in a closed system","C":"It becomes mass","D":"Thermal energy"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-07","spec":"1SC0 Topic 3","stem":"A 2 kg object is lifted 5 m. Using g = 10 N/kg, what is its gravitational potential energy increase?","options":{"A":"Lower field","B":"Less mass","C":"More height","D":"Lower height"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-ENERGY","scaffold":"Gravitational potential energy change = mass × gravitational field strength × height = 2 × 10 × 5 = 100 J.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change increases the gravitational potential energy gained when lifting an object?","options":{"A":"A greater height","B":"A smaller mass","C":"A smaller height","D":"It becomes mass"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-08","spec":"1SC0 Topic 4","stem":"Which feature is common to all transverse waves?","options":{"A":"The number of particles","B":"They cannot be reflected","C":"They require a vacuum","D":"Perpendicular vibrations"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-WAVE","scaffold":"In a transverse wave, the vibrations are at right angles to the direction of energy transfer. Light and water waves are examples.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the frequency of a wave with a period of 0.25 s?","options":{"A":"Its energy is always destroyed","B":"4 Hz","C":"25 Hz","D":"2 Hz"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-09","spec":"1SC0 Topic 4","stem":"What happens when a wave is refracted as it enters a different medium?","options":{"A":"Speed and direction may change","B":"Its energy is always destroyed","C":"Its frequency must become zero","D":"It becomes radioactive"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-WAVE","scaffold":"Refraction occurs because wave speed changes at a boundary. This can change direction; frequency remains set by the source.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the amplitude of a wave most closely related to?","options":{"A":"The time period only","B":"The wave's frequency only","C":"The energy transferred","D":"The number of particles"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-10","spec":"1SC0 Topic 5","stem":"Which type of electromagnetic radiation has the shortest wavelength?","options":{"A":"Radio waves","B":"Gamma rays","C":"Ultraviolet","D":"Infrared"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-EM","scaffold":"Gamma rays have the shortest wavelength and highest frequency in the electromagnetic spectrum. They also carry the most energy per photon.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies the idea to this case: Which type of electromagnetic radiation has the shortest wavelength?","options":{"A":"Gamma rays","B":"Alpha","C":"Microwaves","D":"X-rays only"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-11","spec":"1SC0 Topic 5","stem":"Why does a converging lens bring parallel rays to a focus?","options":{"A":"The exact colour of every extinct organism","B":"It absorbs all light","C":"It refracts rays toward a focal point","D":"It converts light to sound"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-OPTICS","scaffold":"A converging lens refracts rays so that parallel rays meet at the principal focus. The focal length is the distance from the lens to that point.","reforge":{"stem":"What type of image can a converging lens form when an object is beyond 2F?","options":{"A":"Real, inverted and smaller","B":"Virtual, upright and larger","C":"Real, upright and larger","D":"No image"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-12","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6","stem":"What does the half-life of a radioactive isotope describe?","options":{"A":"The time for all nuclei to decay","B":"The time for radiation to stop","C":"The age of the sample","D":"Half the nuclei decay"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-HALFLIFE","scaffold":"Half-life is the time taken for the number of undecayed nuclei, or the activity, to halve. Decay is random for individual nuclei.","reforge":{"stem":"Which radiation is the most ionising?","options":{"A":"Visible light","B":"Alpha","C":"Gamma","D":"Beta"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-13","spec":"1SC0 Topic 2","stem":"A runner changes velocity from 4 m/s to 10 m/s in 3 s. What is the acceleration?","options":{"A":"2 m/s2","B":"3 m/s²","C":"6 m/s²","D":"14 m/s²"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-MOTION","scaffold":"Acceleration = change in velocity ÷ time = (10 − 4) ÷ 3 = 2 m/s².","reforge":{"stem":"What does negative acceleration mean?","options":{"A":"Mass becomes negative","B":"The object has no velocity","C":"Negative-direction change","D":"No force acts"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-14","spec":"1SC0 Topic 2","stem":"What does the area under a velocity–time graph represent?","options":{"A":"Acceleration only","B":"Distance","C":"14 m/s²","D":"Resultant force"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-MOTION","scaffold":"The area under a velocity–time graph gives distance travelled. For a rectangle, calculate base × height; for a triangle, use half × base × height.","reforge":{"stem":"What does the gradient of a velocity–time graph show?","options":{"A":"Energy transferred","B":"1,800 m/s","C":"Energy","D":"Acceleration"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-15","spec":"1SC0 Topic 2","stem":"Why does a longer stopping distance increase collision risk?","options":{"A":"The brakes create extra momentum in the moving vehicle","B":"The vehicle loses its mass","C":"There is more distance for energy to be dissipated","D":"The road removes all forces"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-STOPPING-DISTANCE","scaffold":"A moving vehicle has kinetic energy. Brakes and other resistive forces need distance and time to transfer that energy, so a longer stopping distance increases risk.","reforge":{"stem":"What can increase thinking distance?","options":{"A":"Reaction time/speed","B":"Better concentration","C":"Dry road surface","D":"Brighter headlights only"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-16","spec":"1SC0 Topic 2","stem":"What happens when a falling object reaches terminal velocity?","options":{"A":"The object reverses direction","B":"Weight becomes zero","C":"Acceleration increases forever","D":"Balanced resistive force"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-NEWTON","scaffold":"As speed rises, air resistance increases. At terminal velocity, air resistance equals weight, so the resultant force and acceleration are zero.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does a parachutist slow after opening a parachute?","options":{"A":"Weight disappears","B":"More air drag","C":"Gravity reverses","D":"Mass falls to zero"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-17","spec":"1SC0 Topic 3","stem":"What is the efficiency of a device?","options":{"A":"Useful output / input","B":"Total input divided by time only","C":"Wasted energy divided by mass","D":"Useful force multiplied by distance only"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-ENERGY","scaffold":"Efficiency = useful output energy ÷ total input energy. It can be expressed as a decimal or multiplied by 100 for a percentage.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can no real device be 100% efficient?","options":{"A":"Input energy is always zero","B":"A smaller height","C":"Dissipated energy","D":"Useful output cannot exist"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-18","spec":"1SC0 Topic 3","stem":"A machine transfers 900 J in 30 s. What is its power?","options":{"A":"270 W","B":"30 W","C":"870 W","D":"27,000 W"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-POWER","scaffold":"Power = energy transferred ÷ time = 900 ÷ 30 = 30 W.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a high power rating indicate?","options":{"A":"The device must be 100% efficient","B":"Less energy can ever be used","C":"The device has no resistance","D":"Energy per second"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-19","spec":"1SC0 Topic 5","stem":"What is the law of reflection?","options":{"A":"The reflected ray has no direction","B":"All rays are absorbed","C":"Equal angles","D":"Angles are measured from the surface"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-OPTICS","scaffold":"The angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection, and both angles are measured from the normal line at the surface.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does a plane mirror form a virtual image?","options":{"A":"Rays appear behind mirror","B":"Light stops at the mirror","C":"The image is made of sound","D":"The mirror creates new matter"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-20","spec":"1SC0 Topic 4","stem":"A wave has frequency 5 Hz and wavelength 2 m. What is its speed?","options":{"A":"0.4 m/s","B":"2.5 m/s","C":"7 m/s","D":"10 m/s"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-WAVE","scaffold":"Wave speed = frequency × wavelength = 5 × 2 = 10 m/s.","reforge":{"stem":"What happens to wave frequency when the source oscillates faster?","options":{"A":"25 Hz","B":"Higher frequency","C":"It must halve","D":"It becomes a mass"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-21","spec":"1SC0 Topic 5","stem":"Which type of electromagnetic radiation is used to sterilise some medical equipment?","options":{"A":"Gamma rays","B":"Radio waves","C":"Microwaves","D":"Infrared"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-EM","scaffold":"Gamma radiation is penetrating and can kill microorganisms. Its ionising nature means it must be used with appropriate safety controls.","reforge":{"stem":"Which electromagnetic radiation is used for satellite communication?","options":{"A":"Ultraviolet","B":"Alpha","C":"Microwaves","D":"X-rays only"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-22","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6","stem":"A radioactive sample has an activity of 800 Bq and a half-life of 2 hours. What is its activity after 6 hours?","options":{"A":"200 Bq","B":"100 Bq","C":"400 Bq","D":"50 Bq"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-HALFLIFE","scaffold":"Six hours contains three half-lives: 800 → 400 → 200 → 100 Bq.","reforge":{"stem":"After two half-lives, what fraction of the original nuclei remains?","options":{"A":"All of them","B":"One half","C":"One eighth","D":"One quarter"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-23","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6","stem":"Why are sources of ionising radiation kept in shielded containers?","options":{"A":"To stop all radioactive decay","B":"To increase their half-life","C":"Reduce exposure","D":"To make them non-radioactive"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-RADIATION","scaffold":"Shielding absorbs or reduces radiation reaching people. Distance, exposure time and suitable shielding are key safety controls.","reforge":{"stem":"Which radiation is most easily stopped by paper?","options":{"A":"Alpha","B":"Beta","C":"Gamma","D":"Microwaves"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS1-24","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6","stem":"What happens to the mass number during alpha decay?","options":{"A":"Gamma","B":"It increases by 4","C":"It stays the same","D":"Minus 4"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-RADIATION","scaffold":"An alpha particle contains two protons and two neutrons. Emitting one reduces the parent nucleus's mass number by four and atomic number by two.","reforge":{"stem":"What is an alpha particle?","options":{"A":"A fast electron","B":"Helium nucleus","C":"A photon","D":"A neutron only"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-003","stem":"What is the main role of ribosomes in a cell?","options":{"A":"Making proteins","B":"Controlling osmosis","C":"Releasing energy","D":"Storing genetic material"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-RIBO","scaffold":"Ribosomes are the sites of protein synthesis. Mitochondria release energy, while the nucleus contains most of the genetic material.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which structure is the main site of aerobic respiration?","options":{"A":"Nucleus","B":"Ribosome","C":"Mitochondrion","D":"Controlling osmosis"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-009","stem":"Why are antibiotics ineffective against viruses?","options":{"A":"Viruses are too large to enter","B":"No bacterial drug target","C":"Viruses have thicker cell walls","D":"Viruses can digest antibiotics"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-DISEASE","scaffold":"Antibiotics target bacterial structures or processes. Viruses reproduce inside host cells and do not have the same cellular targets.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What is one reason new antibiotic-resistant strains can spread quickly?","options":{"A":"All bacteria become viruses","B":"Antibiotics create useful organs","C":"Resistance stops bacterial reproduction","D":"Resistance can be inherited by bacteria"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-015","stem":"What happens to an enzyme's active site when it is denatured?","options":{"A":"It gains a nucleus","B":"It becomes a substrate","C":"Shape changes","D":"It produces more heat"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-ENZYME","scaffold":"High temperature or extreme pH can disrupt bonds that hold an enzyme's shape. The active site changes so the substrate no longer fits effectively.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Why does an enzyme-controlled reaction slow at a very low temperature?","options":{"A":"Low particle energy","B":"The enzyme is always denatured","C":"A lower substrate concentration","D":"The active site disappears permanently"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-021","stem":"Why are antibodies specific to particular pathogens?","options":{"A":"They are made by red blood cells","B":"They contain no proteins","C":"They attack every cell equally","D":"Matching antigen sites"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-DEFENCE","scaffold":"An antibody has a complementary binding site for a particular antigen. This allows it to bind to the pathogen or toxin and help target it for destruction.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What do white blood cells produce after recognising a foreign antigen?","options":{"A":"Starch granules","B":"Specific antibodies","C":"More red blood cells only","D":"Bile salts"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-027","stem":"Which change is a physical change?","options":{"A":"Melting ice","B":"The beaker","C":"Burning methane","D":"Salt"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-STATES","scaffold":"Melting changes the state of water but does not make a new substance. Chemical changes form new substances with different properties.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"During boiling, what happens to the particles?","options":{"A":"They all become gases","B":"They become new atoms","C":"Particles spread apart","D":"They form a new compound"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-033","stem":"Why can carbon be used to extract copper from copper oxide?","options":{"A":"Every individual gains a useful mutation","B":"Carbon is more reactive than copper","C":"Aluminium has no melting point","D":"Copper cannot form ions"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-EXTRACT","scaffold":"Carbon is above copper in the reactivity series, so it can remove oxygen from copper oxide in a reduction reaction.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What is the main raw material for extracting iron in a blast furnace?","options":{"A":"No carbon dioxide is produced anywhere","B":"Pure iron metal","C":"Carbon is more reactive than copper","D":"Iron oxide ore"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-039","stem":"what is the empirical formula of a compound with a 1:2 ratio of carbon to oxygen atoms?","options":{"A":"CO","B":"C2O","C":"CO2","D":"C2O2"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-FORMULA","scaffold":"The empirical formula gives the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms. A carbon-to-oxygen ratio of 1:2 is written CO2.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What does a molecular formula show?","options":{"A":"Actual atom numbers","B":"Only the simplest ratio","C":"The reaction rate","D":"The melting point"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-045","stem":"What is the effect of increasing pressure on an equilibrium with more gas molecules on the reactant side?","options":{"A":"It stops the forward reaction","B":"It always stops","C":"It has no effect on gases","D":"Fewer gas molecules"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-EQUIL","scaffold":"Increasing pressure favours the side with fewer gas molecules because this reduces the pressure change. The outcome depends on the equation.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What happens if both sides of a gas equilibrium have the same number of molecules?","options":{"A":"Reactants always form","B":"No pressure shift","C":"Products always form","D":"The catalyst is removed"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-051","stem":"What does the gradient of a distance–time graph represent? (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"Speed","B":"Acceleration","C":"35 m","D":"Energy"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-MOTION","scaffold":"The gradient is change in distance divided by change in time, which is speed. A steeper line represents a greater speed.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What does a horizontal section on a distance–time graph show?","options":{"A":"Mass becomes negative","B":"Energy transferred","C":"The object is stationary","D":"The object is moving fastest"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"GCSE-PHASE6-PHYS1-01","stem":"The gradient of a velocity–time graph represents: ","options":{"A":"distance travelled","B":"acceleration","C":"force divided by mass only","D":"power"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-MOTION","scaffold":"Acceleration is the change in velocity per unit time, shown by the gradient.","reforge":{"stem":"In a practical example, which answer fits? The gradient of a velocity–time graph represents: ","options":{"A":"Acceleration only","B":"acceleration","C":"14 m/s²","D":"Energy"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"},{"id":"GCSE-PHASE6-PHYS1-02","stem":"During radioactive decay, the activity of a sample: ","options":{"A":"increases at a constant rate","B":"becomes negative immediately","C":"decreases over time but may never reach exactly zero","D":"stays exactly identical for every isotope of the element"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-HALFLIFE","scaffold":"Random decay produces an overall exponential decrease in activity.","reforge":{"stem":"A worked example tests the same principle: During radioactive decay, the activity of a sample: ","options":{"A":"The time for all nuclei to decay","B":"The time for radiation to stop","C":"decreases over time but may never reach exactly zero","D":"The brakes create extra momentum in the moving vehicle"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-1"}]},"GCSE-SCI-BIO-2":{"label":"Biology 2 — Plants, Coordination, Transport & Ecosystems","color":"#15803d","questions":[{"id":"SCI-BIO2-01","spec":"1SC0 Topic 1","stem":"What is the main role of chlorophyll in photosynthesis?","options":{"A":"Absorbing light energy","B":"Releasing carbon dioxide","C":"A type of root tissue","D":"A hormone in animals"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-PHOTO","scaffold":"Chlorophyll absorbs light energy, which is used to drive the reactions that make glucose from carbon dioxide and water.","reforge":{"stem":"Which gas is taken in by a leaf for photosynthesis?","options":{"A":"The plant loses all chlorophyll","B":"A product that is never used","C":"Carbon dioxide","D":"Hydrogen"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-02","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6","stem":"Why are root hair cells effective at absorbing water and mineral ions?","options":{"A":"They contain no cytoplasm","B":"Large surface area","C":"They are covered by wax","D":"They have no cell membrane"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-ROOT-HAIR","scaffold":"Root hair cells have a long extension that provides a large surface area. Their membranes and transport proteins help absorb water and mineral ions from the soil.","reforge":{"stem":"How does water enter a root hair cell?","options":{"A":"By active transport only","B":"By transpiration from the root","C":"By digestion","D":"By osmosis"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-03","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6","stem":"What is the function of xylem tissue?","options":{"A":"Making pollen grains","B":"Transporting sugars from leaves","C":"Water and minerals","D":"Controlling stomata only"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-PLANTS","scaffold":"Xylem vessels carry water and mineral ions from roots towards the leaves. They are strengthened with lignin and form continuous tubes.","reforge":{"stem":"Which tissue transports sucrose around a plant?","options":{"A":"Phloem","B":"Xylem","C":"Epidermis","D":"Meristem"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-04","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6","stem":"Why does transpiration usually increase when temperature rises?","options":{"A":"Roots stop absorbing ions","B":"The leaf makes less glucose","C":"Stomata always close","D":"More kinetic energy"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-TRANSPIRATION","scaffold":"Higher temperature increases evaporation from mesophyll cells and diffusion of water vapour through stomata, increasing the transpiration rate if other factors are constant.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the effect of strong wind on transpiration?","options":{"A":"It stops evaporation completely","B":"Removes humid air","C":"It reduces the diffusion gradient","D":"It closes every stomata"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-05","spec":"1SC0 Topic 7","stem":"Which part of the nervous system carries impulses from receptors to the central nervous system?","options":{"A":"Sensory","B":"Relay neurone","C":"Motor neurone","D":"Effector"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-NERVOUS","scaffold":"Sensory neurones carry impulses from receptors towards the central nervous system. Motor neurones carry impulses to effectors.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the role of a synapse?","options":{"A":"It connects bones","B":"It pumps blood","C":"Signal transfer","D":"It produces antibodies"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-06","spec":"1SC0 Topic 7","stem":"What is a reflex action?","options":{"A":"A slow conscious decision","B":"A rapid automatic response","C":"A response that needs no receptor","D":"A learned movement only"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-NERVOUS","scaffold":"Reflexes are rapid and automatic. They protect the body and are coordinated through a reflex arc, often without conscious involvement first.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are reflex actions useful?","options":{"A":"They delay responses","B":"They prevent all movement","C":"They require detailed thought","D":"Reduces injury"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-07","spec":"1SC0 Topic 7","stem":"How does insulin help regulate blood glucose concentration?","options":{"A":"It prevents cells using glucose","B":"It converts glycogen into glucose","C":"More glucose uptake","D":"It raises blood glucose directly"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-FEEDBACK","scaffold":"When blood glucose rises, insulin causes cells to take up more glucose and promotes conversion of glucose to glycogen in the liver and muscles.","reforge":{"stem":"What happens when blood glucose becomes too low?","options":{"A":"Glucose release","B":"Insulin always increases","C":"The pancreas stops working","D":"Glycogen becomes more stored"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-08","spec":"1SC0 Topic 8","stem":"Why do mammals need a double circulatory system?","options":{"A":"It sends all blood to the lungs twice","B":"It prevents oxygen entering blood","C":"It removes the need for a heart","D":"It keeps blood at suitable pressure"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"The right side pumps blood to the lungs at lower pressure, while the left side pumps oxygenated blood to the body at higher pressure. This supports efficient delivery.","reforge":{"stem":"Which chamber pumps oxygenated blood to the body?","options":{"A":"Right atrium","B":"Left ventricle","C":"Left atrium","D":"Right ventricle"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-09","spec":"1SC0 Topic 8","stem":"How are alveoli adapted for gas exchange?","options":{"A":"Thin walls, large area","B":"They have thick dry walls","C":"They contain cartilage rings","D":"They prevent diffusion"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-GASEXCHANGE","scaffold":"Many alveoli provide a large surface area. Their walls are one cell thick and have a good blood supply, maintaining steep concentration gradients.","reforge":{"stem":"What process moves oxygen from alveoli into the blood?","options":{"A":"Active transport","B":"Osmosis","C":"Diffusion","D":"Filtration"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-10","spec":"1SC0 Topic 9","stem":"Why is energy transferred between trophic levels inefficient?","options":{"A":"Producers absorb all energy","B":"Uneaten or undigested material","C":"Consumers never respire","D":"Energy is created by decomposers"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-FOODCHAIN","scaffold":"Energy is lost through uneaten material, waste, movement and heat released by respiration. Only a fraction becomes biomass available to the next level.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the original energy source for most food chains?","options":{"A":"Mineral ions","B":"Nitrate fertiliser","C":"Decomposer heat","D":"Light from the Sun"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-11","spec":"1SC0 Topic 9","stem":"What is the role of decomposers in the carbon cycle?","options":{"A":"They make fossil fuels instantly","B":"They remove all carbon from soil","C":"Respiratory carbon dioxide","D":"They stop decay"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-CYCLES","scaffold":"Decomposers break down dead material and waste. Their respiration returns carbon dioxide to the atmosphere while nutrients are recycled into soil.","reforge":{"stem":"Which process removes carbon dioxide from the atmosphere?","options":{"A":"Photosynthesis","B":"They remove all minerals","C":"Combustion","D":"Decay"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-12","spec":"1SC0 Topic 9","stem":"Why can fertiliser runoff cause fish deaths in a lake?","options":{"A":"It makes every fish produce more oxygen","B":"It removes all minerals from water","C":"It stops bacteria decomposing","D":"Algae and oxygen loss"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-EUTROPHICATION","scaffold":"Nitrates can cause algal blooms. When algae die, decomposers respire while breaking them down, reducing dissolved oxygen and causing fish to suffocate.","reforge":{"stem":"What is eutrophication?","options":{"A":"The natural formation of a new species","B":"Nutrient enrichment","C":"A decrease in all plant growth","D":"The removal of carbon from air"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-13","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–9","stem":"What is a limiting factor in photosynthesis?","options":{"A":"A condition that restricts the rate","B":"Every individual gains a useful mutation","C":"A type of root tissue","D":"A hormone in animals"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-PHOTO","scaffold":"Light intensity, carbon dioxide concentration and temperature can limit photosynthesis. The limiting factor is the factor in shortest effective supply at a particular time.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does increasing light intensity eventually stop increasing photosynthesis?","options":{"A":"Carbon dioxide becomes oxygen","B":"The plant loses all chlorophyll","C":"Another limiting factor","D":"The roots stop existing"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-14","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–9","stem":"What is the role of stomata in a leaf?","options":{"A":"They make mineral ions","B":"Gas exchange","C":"They pump blood","D":"They digest starch"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-TRANSPIRATION","scaffold":"Stomata are pores that allow carbon dioxide to enter and oxygen and water vapour to leave. Guard cells regulate their opening.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might stomata close during a very dry period?","options":{"A":"To absorb more sunlight","B":"To increase transpiration","C":"To stop all respiration","D":"Reduce water loss"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-15","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–9","stem":"What is the function of auxin in a plant shoot?","options":{"A":"It digests proteins","B":"It carries oxygen in blood","C":"Auxin-driven elongation","D":"It lowers every response"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-AUXIN","scaffold":"Auxin is a plant hormone. Unequal auxin distribution can cause shoots to bend towards light because cells on one side elongate more.","reforge":{"stem":"What is phototropism?","options":{"A":"Light response","B":"Transport of sugars in phloem","C":"The breakdown of starch","D":"A disease of roots"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-16","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–9","stem":"What is the role of the kidneys in homeostasis?","options":{"A":"They absorb light energy","B":"They pump blood around the body","C":"They produce bile for digestion","D":"Water and ions"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-HOMEOSTASIS","scaffold":"The kidneys filter blood and selectively reabsorb useful substances. They help control water balance, ion concentration and urea excretion.","reforge":{"stem":"Which waste substance is removed from the blood by the kidneys?","options":{"A":"Oxygen","B":"Urea","C":"Glucose in all conditions","D":"Starch"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-17","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–9","stem":"How does the body cool itself when core temperature rises?","options":{"A":"Sweat evaporation and skin vasodilation","B":"Trees create carbon dioxide by absorbing light","C":"Reduced blood flow to skin","D":"Increased insulation only"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-FEEDBACK","scaffold":"Sweating increases evaporative cooling, while vasodilation sends more blood near the skin surface. Both increase heat transfer to the surroundings.","reforge":{"stem":"What is vasodilation?","options":{"A":"Production of antibodies","B":"Narrowing of skin blood vessels","C":"Widened skin vessels","D":"Contraction of the diaphragm"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-18","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–9","stem":"Why does the heart muscle need its own coronary blood supply?","options":{"A":"To carry urine to the kidneys","B":"Oxygen and glucose supply","C":"To produce digestive enzymes","D":"To cool the lungs only"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Cardiac muscle contracts continuously and needs aerobic respiration. Coronary arteries deliver oxygen and glucose; blocked arteries can reduce this supply.","reforge":{"stem":"What can a blocked coronary artery cause?","options":{"A":"A larger lung surface area","B":"More oxygen reaching the muscle","C":"Faster digestion","D":"Less oxygen to heart"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-19","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–9","stem":"Why does exercise increase breathing rate?","options":{"A":"To reduce blood flow to muscles","B":"To stop respiration","C":"More oxygen, less CO2","D":"To make glucose from nitrogen"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-GASEXCHANGE","scaffold":"Active muscles respire faster and need more oxygen. Increased ventilation also removes the additional carbon dioxide produced.","reforge":{"stem":"Why may breathing remain fast after vigorous exercise?","options":{"A":"Oxygen debt","B":"The lungs have stopped gas exchange","C":"The muscles need no energy","D":"Carbon dioxide is no longer produced"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-20","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–9","stem":"What is a quadrat used to investigate?","options":{"A":"The speed of nerve impulses","B":"The blood pressure of a mammal","C":"The pH of a cell","D":"Organism distribution"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-ECOLOGY","scaffold":"Quadrats sample plants or slow-moving organisms. Random placement can reduce bias when estimating abundance or distribution across a habitat.","reforge":{"stem":"Why should quadrats be placed randomly?","options":{"A":"To guarantee identical results","B":"Less sampling bias","C":"To avoid recording data","D":"To increase the temperature"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-21","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–9","stem":"What is carrying capacity?","options":{"A":"Sustainable population","B":"The number of species on Earth","C":"The mass of one organism","D":"The speed of decomposition"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-ECOLOGY","scaffold":"Food, space, water, disease and predation can limit population size. Carrying capacity is not fixed if environmental conditions change.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change could lower a habitat's carrying capacity?","options":{"A":"A stable water supply","B":"More nesting space","C":"Less food","D":"Reduced competition"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-22","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–9","stem":"Why are decomposers important in ecosystems?","options":{"A":"They remove all minerals","B":"Nutrient recycling","C":"They prevent respiration","D":"They produce sunlight"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-CYCLES","scaffold":"Bacteria and fungi secrete enzymes onto dead material and absorb soluble products. This returns mineral ions to the soil and supports plant growth.","reforge":{"stem":"What process releases mineral ions from dead organisms?","options":{"A":"Transpiration","B":"Photosynthesis","C":"Pollination","D":"Decay"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-23","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–9","stem":"Why can deforestation increase atmospheric carbon dioxide?","options":{"A":"Soil stops containing minerals","B":"Trees create carbon dioxide by absorbing light","C":"Less CO2 uptake","D":"All animals stop respiring"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-CARBON","scaffold":"Removing trees reduces photosynthetic carbon dioxide uptake. Burning or decomposing the cleared biomass can also release stored carbon.","reforge":{"stem":"Which action can increase carbon storage in a habitat?","options":{"A":"Tree planting","B":"Burning woodland","C":"Draining every wetland","D":"Removing vegetation"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"SCI-BIO2-24","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–9","stem":"Why can a food web be more stable than a single food chain?","options":{"A":"It has only one species","B":"It contains no competition","C":"It prevents energy loss","D":"Alternative feeding links"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-FOODCHAIN","scaffold":"If one food source falls, a consumer may use another link in a food web. This can reduce the impact of a change, although ecosystems remain vulnerable to major disturbances.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a food web show?","options":{"A":"Only one sequence of feeding","B":"Feeding relationships","C":"The mass of every organism","D":"The age of each habitat"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-004","stem":"Why does mitosis produce two genetically identical daughter cells?","options":{"A":"DNA is copied once before one division","B":"It makes every fish produce more oxygen","C":"The chromosome number is halved","D":"The cells combine after division"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-MITOSIS","scaffold":"Before mitosis, DNA is replicated. The copied chromosomes are then separated so each daughter cell receives an identical set.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What is one function of mitosis in a multicellular organism?","options":{"A":"Producing gametes","B":"Creating genetic variation","C":"Growth and tissue repair","D":"Reducing chromosome number"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-010","stem":"What is the purpose of a control group in a drug trial?","options":{"A":"To receive the largest dose","B":"To provide a comparison","C":"To guarantee a positive result","D":"To remove all variables"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-TRIAL","scaffold":"A control group provides a comparison with the treatment group. A placebo can help reduce the effects of expectation when appropriate.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Why are double-blind trials useful?","options":{"A":"They eliminate every risk","B":"They make a placebo active","C":"They prevent data collection","D":"Less trial bias"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-016","stem":"Which reagent is used to test a food sample for starch?","options":{"A":"Ethanol","B":"Biuret solution","C":"Iodine solution","D":"Benedict's solution"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-FOOD","scaffold":"Iodine solution turns blue-black when starch is present. Biuret tests protein and Benedict's solution can test reducing sugars after heating.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What colour indicates protein in a positive Biuret test?","options":{"A":"Lilac","B":"Blue-black","C":"Brick-red","D":"Orange"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-022","stem":"Why is a placebo used in some clinical trials?","options":{"A":"It contains the highest dose","B":"It replaces ethical approval","C":"It guarantees the drug works","D":"Treatment comparison"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-TRIAL","scaffold":"A placebo looks like the treatment but lacks its active ingredient. Comparing responses helps identify effects caused by the drug rather than expectation.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Why should a clinical trial use a large sample where possible?","options":{"A":"It prevents all variation","B":"Representative results","C":"It guarantees no side effects","D":"It removes the need for controls"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-028","stem":"Which method separates a soluble solid from a solution?","options":{"A":"Evaporation or crystallisation","B":"Titration","C":"They have different element symbols","D":"Distillation only"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-SEPARATION","scaffold":"Evaporation removes solvent and crystallisation can obtain the dissolved solid. Filtration separates an insoluble solid from a liquid.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which technique separates different dyes in an ink sample?","options":{"A":"Distillation only","B":"Filtration alone","C":"Chromatography","D":"Precipitation"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-034","stem":"Why is electrolysis used to extract aluminium rather than carbon reduction?","options":{"A":"Direct current changes direction constantly","B":"Aluminium is more reactive than carbon","C":"High electricity demand","D":"Carbon cannot conduct electricity"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-EXTRACT","scaffold":"Aluminium is more reactive than carbon, so its oxide is not reduced by carbon. Electrolysis is needed, although it uses considerable energy.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What is one environmental cost of extracting aluminium by electrolysis?","options":{"A":"Copper carbonate","B":"No carbon dioxide is produced anywhere","C":"The ore becomes radioactive","D":"High electricity demand"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-040","stem":"what happens to the particles of a solute when it dissolves?","options":{"A":"They all become gases","B":"They become new elements","C":"Particles spread out","D":"They stop existing"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-STATES","scaffold":"Dissolving separates solute particles and disperses them among solvent particles. No new substance is made by this physical process.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What is the solvent in a salt solution made with water?","options":{"A":"Water","B":"Salt","C":"The beaker","D":"The air above it"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-046","stem":"For an endothermic forward reaction, what does increasing temperature generally do to the equilibrium yield of products?","options":{"A":"It always decreases the rate","B":"It removes reactants","C":"It has no effect","D":"Higher product yield"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-EQUIL2","scaffold":"An endothermic forward reaction absorbs heat. Increasing temperature favours the direction that absorbs the added heat, increasing the equilibrium product yield.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What is the energy transfer in an endothermic reaction?","options":{"A":"No energy changes","B":"Energy enters","C":"Energy is destroyed","D":"Energy leaves to the surroundings"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-052","stem":"A resultant force acts on an object. What must happen?","options":{"A":"It accelerates","B":"F = m ÷ a","C":"It becomes stationary","D":"Its mass changes"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-NEWTON","scaffold":"A non-zero resultant force causes acceleration. The acceleration may be a change in speed, direction or both.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What is the resultant force when an object moves at constant velocity?","options":{"A":"7 N","B":"Greater than its mass","C":"Zero","D":"It accelerates"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"GCSE-PHASE6-BIO2-01","stem":"Transpiration is the loss of water vapour mainly from: ","options":{"A":"roots through xylem only","B":"leaves through stomata","C":"seeds through chloroplasts","D":"flowers through phloem"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-TRANSPIRATION","scaffold":"Water vapour diffuses out through open stomata in leaves.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies the idea to this case: Transpiration is the loss of water vapour mainly from: ","options":{"A":"leaves through stomata","B":"Releasing carbon dioxide","C":"Stomata always close","D":"They digest starch"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"},{"id":"GCSE-PHASE6-BIO2-02","stem":"A food web is useful because it shows: ","options":{"A":"only one linear energy transfer","B":"the exact population size of every species in the habitat","C":"interconnected feeding relationships in a community","D":"the chemical formula of glucose"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-FOODCHAIN","scaffold":"Food webs show that organisms may have several prey and predators.","reforge":{"stem":"In a practical example, which answer fits? A food web is useful because it shows: ","options":{"A":"Energy is destroyed by friction between the surfaces","B":"interconnected feeding relationships in a community","C":"Energy is created by decomposers in the soil community","D":"Only one sequence of feeding"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-bio-2"}]},"GCSE-SCI-CHEM-2":{"label":"Chemistry 2 — Groups, Rates, Energy, Fuels & Earth Science","color":"#c2410c","questions":[{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-01","spec":"1SC0 Topic 1","stem":"Why do elements in the same group have similar chemical properties?","options":{"A":"Same outer electrons","B":"They have the same mass number","C":"Chlorine is a metal","D":"It always increases"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-GROUPS","scaffold":"Elements in a group have the same number of electrons in their outer shell. This gives them similar patterns of chemical reactivity.","reforge":{"stem":"What happens to reactivity down Group 1?","options":{"A":"It stays identical","B":"It always becomes zero","C":"It generally increases","D":"It generally decreases"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-02","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6","stem":"What observation is expected when chlorine water is added to potassium bromide solution?","options":{"A":"The solution becomes colourless","B":"Orange-brown","C":"A blue precipitate forms","D":"Hydrogen is released"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-GROUPS","scaffold":"Chlorine is more reactive than bromine and displaces bromine from bromide ions. The dissolved bromine gives an orange-brown colour.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can chlorine displace bromide ions?","options":{"A":"Bromide ions are positive","B":"Bromine has no electrons","C":"Chlorine is a metal","D":"More reactive halogen"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-03","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6","stem":"What is a characteristic of the noble gases?","options":{"A":"They have one outer electron","B":"They react violently with water","C":"Full outer shells","D":"They form acidic metals"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-GROUPS","scaffold":"Noble gases have stable electron arrangements, so they are very unreactive under ordinary conditions.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is helium used in some balloons?","options":{"A":"Less dense, unreactive","B":"It burns more safely than oxygen","C":"It reacts with rubber","D":"It is denser than water"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-04","spec":"1SC0 Topic 7","stem":"Which change usually increases the rate of a reaction between two solids?","options":{"A":"Removing collisions","B":"Lowering temperature","C":"Reducing surface area","D":"Using smaller pieces"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-SURFACE","scaffold":"Smaller pieces provide a larger surface area, so particles can collide more frequently. The effect is strongest when other variables are controlled.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does a higher temperature usually increase reaction rate?","options":{"A":"Particles collide less often","B":"More above activation energy","C":"The products disappear","D":"The reactants become heavier"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-05","spec":"1SC0 Topic 7","stem":"What does a catalyst do in a chemical reaction?","options":{"A":"Lower-energy pathway","B":"Changes the products permanently","C":"Raises the activation energy","D":"Is used up as a reactant"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-RATE","scaffold":"A catalyst lowers the activation energy by providing another pathway. It is not used up and does not change the equilibrium position.","reforge":{"stem":"How can a reaction rate be measured from a graph?","options":{"A":"By multiplying time by temperature","B":"By reading the final colour only","C":"By finding the initial gradient","D":"By counting atoms in the beaker"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-06","spec":"1SC0 Topic 7","stem":"What is an exothermic reaction?","options":{"A":"It absorbs heat from the surroundings","B":"Energy to surroundings","C":"It always needs a flame","D":"It has no energy change"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ENERGETICS","scaffold":"In an exothermic reaction, the products have less chemical energy than the reactants and energy is transferred to the surroundings, often warming them.","reforge":{"stem":"What happens to the temperature of the surroundings in an exothermic reaction?","options":{"A":"It cannot be measured","B":"It always falls","C":"It becomes absolute zero","D":"It usually rises"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-07","spec":"1SC0 Topic 7","stem":"A reaction transfers 600 J in 20 s. What is its power?","options":{"A":"12,000 W","B":"300 W","C":"30 W","D":"580 W"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-POWER","scaffold":"Power = energy transferred ÷ time = 600 ÷ 20 = 30 W.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the energy change when bonds form?","options":{"A":"Energy is released","B":"Energy is never involved","C":"All energy is destroyed","D":"Only light is created"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-08","spec":"1SC0 Topic 8","stem":"Why is crude oil separated by fractional distillation?","options":{"A":"It removes every impurity by filtration","B":"All fractions have the same formula","C":"It creates new elements","D":"Different boiling points"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-FRACTION","scaffold":"Fractional distillation separates hydrocarbons because their molecules have different boiling points. The fractions have different properties and uses.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a hydrocarbon?","options":{"A":"A carbon-free salt","B":"Carbon-hydrogen compound","C":"They have the same mass number","D":"An oxygen isotope"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-09","spec":"1SC0 Topic 8","stem":"Why is cracking useful in the petrochemical industry?","options":{"A":"Useful shorter hydrocarbons","B":"It turns all fuels into metals","C":"It removes carbon dioxide from air","D":"It produces only water"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-CRACK","scaffold":"Cracking breaks long-chain hydrocarbons into shorter, more useful molecules. It can produce alkenes for polymers as well as smaller alkanes for fuels.","reforge":{"stem":"What test identifies an alkene?","options":{"A":"Universal indicator becomes purple","B":"Limewater turns cloudy","C":"Bromine water becomes colourless","D":"A glowing splint relights immediately"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-10","spec":"1SC0 Topic 8","stem":"What is one environmental concern linked to burning fossil fuels?","options":{"A":"They remove oxygen permanently","B":"CO2 climate effect","C":"They produce no gases","D":"They stop all acid rain"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ATMOS","scaffold":"Combustion of fossil fuels releases carbon dioxide. Increased atmospheric carbon dioxide strengthens the greenhouse effect and can contribute to climate change.","reforge":{"stem":"Which pollutant can cause acid rain?","options":{"A":"Water prevents all reactions","B":"Helium","C":"Nitrogen gas","D":"Sulfur dioxide"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-11","spec":"1SC0 Topic 8","stem":"Why is potable water not necessarily pure water?","options":{"A":"It must contain pathogens","B":"It contains no water molecules","C":"Safe; dissolved substances","D":"It is always distilled"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-WATER","scaffold":"Potable water is treated to be safe to drink, but it can still contain dissolved minerals. Pure water contains only water molecules.","reforge":{"stem":"Which process can make water potable from a fresh water source?","options":{"A":"Filtration and sterilisation","B":"Adding more pathogens","C":"Burning the water","D":"Removing every dissolved gas"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-12","spec":"1SC0 Topic 8","stem":"Why is recycling metals often more sustainable than extracting new ores?","options":{"A":"It makes metals non-conductive","B":"It creates unlimited raw materials","C":"It prevents every emission","D":"Less energy and mining"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-CARBON","scaffold":"Recycling can reduce energy use, mining damage and waste. Its environmental benefit depends on collection, sorting and the energy source used.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one reason aluminium recycling saves energy?","options":{"A":"Ore extraction needs no electricity","B":"Less energy than electrolysis","C":"Aluminium cannot be melted","D":"Recycling removes all transport"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-13","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–8","stem":"Why does Group 1 reactivity increase down the group?","options":{"A":"Outer electron farther","B":"The atoms gain no shells","C":"The nuclei lose all protons","D":"The elements become noble gases"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-GROUPS","scaffold":"Down Group 1, atoms have more electron shells. The outer electron is further from the nucleus and more shielded, so it is lost more easily.","reforge":{"stem":"What do Group 1 metals form when they react?","options":{"A":"Noble gas atoms","B":"Two-negative ions","C":"One-positive ion","D":"Neutral molecules only"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-14","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–8","stem":"What happens to halogen reactivity down Group 7?","options":{"A":"It always increases","B":"Reactivity falls","C":"It becomes identical","D":"It changes into metallic bonding"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-GROUPS","scaffold":"Down Group 7, the outer shell is further from the nucleus and more shielded. Attraction for an incoming electron weakens, so reactivity falls.","reforge":{"stem":"What type of ion does a halogen form?","options":{"A":"No ion ever","B":"A one-positive ion","C":"A two-positive ion","D":"One-negative ion"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-15","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–8","stem":"Why do noble gases have low boiling points?","options":{"A":"They contain hydrogen bonds","B":"They have strong ionic lattices","C":"Weak interatomic forces","D":"They are all solids"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-GROUPS","scaffold":"Noble gases are monatomic and have weak intermolecular forces. Little energy is needed to separate their atoms, so their boiling points are low.","reforge":{"stem":"What happens to noble-gas boiling points down the group?","options":{"A":"Boiling points rise","B":"They become zero","C":"They decrease because atoms shrink","D":"They stay exactly constant"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-16","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–8","stem":"Why does increasing concentration usually increase reaction rate?","options":{"A":"The products become reactants","B":"Particles lose all energy","C":"The activation energy becomes infinite","D":"More collisions"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-RATE","scaffold":"More particles in the same volume increases collision frequency. If enough collisions have energy above activation energy, the reaction proceeds faster.","reforge":{"stem":"What should be controlled when comparing reaction rates?","options":{"A":"The question wording","B":"Control other variables","C":"The identity of every student","D":"The final conclusion"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-17","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–8","stem":"What does an energy profile show?","options":{"A":"Reaction energy profile","B":"The colour of every product","C":"The atomic number only","D":"The volume of a gas only"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ENERGETICS","scaffold":"An energy profile shows reactant and product energy levels and the activation energy. The difference between levels indicates whether the reaction is exothermic or endothermic.","reforge":{"stem":"What is activation energy?","options":{"A":"Energy released after cooling","B":"The energy in every product","C":"Minimum collision energy","D":"The mass of a catalyst"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-18","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–8","stem":"Why is a reaction mixture cooled in an exothermic practical?","options":{"A":"To make the reactants radioactive","B":"Control heat and safety","C":"To stop measuring temperature","D":"To remove all products"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ENERGETICS","scaffold":"Cooling may control the temperature rise and reduce risk. A fair practical also uses an insulated container and records temperature changes consistently.","reforge":{"stem":"What should be plotted to compare energy changes in a temperature practical?","options":{"A":"Time against student name","B":"Mass number against atomic number","C":"Colour against beaker size only","D":"Temperature against condition"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-19","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–8","stem":"Why are alkenes useful for making polymers?","options":{"A":"They cannot react","B":"They contain no carbon","C":"Double bonds open","D":"They are all metals"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-POLYMER","scaffold":"The carbon–carbon double bond in an alkene can open, allowing many molecules to join into a long-chain polymer.","reforge":{"stem":"What is formed when many alkene molecules join?","options":{"A":"Polymer","B":"A metal oxide","C":"A salt solution","D":"A noble gas"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-20","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–8","stem":"Why does incomplete combustion produce carbon monoxide?","options":{"A":"Water prevents all reactions","B":"There is too much nitrogen","C":"The fuel contains no carbon","D":"Limited oxygen"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ATMOS","scaffold":"When oxygen supply is limited, carbon may be only partially oxidised, producing carbon monoxide rather than carbon dioxide. Carbon monoxide is toxic.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is carbon monoxide dangerous?","options":{"A":"It makes blood contain more oxygen","B":"Reduced oxygen carriage","C":"It is always harmless at any concentration","D":"It destroys all water molecules"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-21","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–8","stem":"What is one benefit of using biofuels?","options":{"A":"Recent plant carbon","B":"They produce no emissions","C":"They require no land","D":"They cannot be burned"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-CARBON","scaffold":"Biofuels can be partly renewable because plant growth absorbs carbon dioxide. Their overall impact depends on land use, transport, processing and farming methods.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a possible disadvantage of large-scale biofuel production?","options":{"A":"It needs no water","B":"It contains no carbon","C":"Food-land competition","D":"It prevents deforestation automatically"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-22","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–8","stem":"Why is the atmosphere's carbon dioxide concentration important?","options":{"A":"It determines atomic number","B":"Greenhouse effect","C":"It prevents all evaporation","D":"It is the only atmospheric gas"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ATMOS","scaffold":"Carbon dioxide absorbs some outgoing infrared radiation. Changes in its concentration can alter the greenhouse effect and global temperature.","reforge":{"stem":"Which gas is a greenhouse gas?","options":{"A":"Helium only","B":"Argon only","C":"Oxygen only","D":"CO2"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-23","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–8","stem":"Why is distillation used to produce pure water from a salt solution?","options":{"A":"The salt becomes a gas","B":"Salt evaporates first","C":"Water distils","D":"The water is destroyed"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-WATER","scaffold":"Distillation boils the solvent and condenses its vapour. Dissolved salts do not evaporate with the water, so they remain in the original flask.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is desalination energy-intensive?","options":{"A":"Heat or pressure needed","B":"Salt creates energy","C":"Water cannot be separated","D":"The process uses no equipment"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"SCI-CHEM2-24","spec":"1SC0 Topic 6–8","stem":"Why should recycled materials be sorted before processing?","options":{"A":"It increases contamination","B":"Sorting creates new atoms","C":"All materials melt identically","D":"Different treatment"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-CARBON","scaffold":"Sorting improves the quality of recycled material and allows suitable processes for metals, glass, paper or polymers. Contamination can reduce the value of the output.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one limitation of recycling plastic?","options":{"A":"All plastics have identical properties","B":"Separate polymer types","C":"Plastic cannot be used again","D":"Recycling needs no energy"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-005","stem":"What is meant by the term allele?","options":{"A":"A version of a gene","B":"100%","C":"A complete chromosome set","D":"A visible characteristic"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-GENETICS","scaffold":"An allele is an alternative form of a gene. Different alleles can contribute to variation in an inherited characteristic.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What is the term for an organism's observable characteristic?","options":{"A":"No alleles for that gene","B":"75%","C":"Phenotype","D":"Gamete"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-011","stem":"What is vaccination intended to produce?","options":{"A":"More pathogenic microbes","B":"Memory cells that respond rapidly","C":"A permanent rise in body temperature","D":"Immediate destruction of every pathogen"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-IMMUNITY","scaffold":"Vaccination exposes the immune system to harmless antigens. Memory cells can then trigger a faster, stronger response if the pathogen is encountered later.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Why may a booster vaccination be needed?","options":{"A":"To prevent antibody production","B":"To remove all white blood cells","C":"To make the pathogen reproduce","D":"Renew immune memory"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-017","stem":"Which base pairs with adenine in a DNA molecule?","options":{"A":"Uracil","B":"Cytosine","C":"Thymine","D":"Another adenine"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-DNA","scaffold":"In DNA, adenine pairs with thymine and cytosine pairs with guanine. Complementary pairing helps DNA copy accurately.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What is the role of DNA in a cell?","options":{"A":"Genetic instructions","B":"It releases all cell energy","C":"It dissolves cell walls","D":"It prevents protein formation"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-023","stem":"What can fossils provide evidence for?","options":{"A":"The absence of mutation","B":"The exact colour of every extinct organism","C":"A complete record of all life","D":"Evolution and ancestry"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-EVOLUTION","scaffold":"Fossils can show how organisms changed over time and may reveal similarities between species. The fossil record is incomplete, so conclusions have limits.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Why is the fossil record incomplete?","options":{"A":"All fossils dissolve instantly","B":"Decay without fossils","C":"Only living animals can fossilise","D":"Scientists cannot compare fossils"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-029","stem":"What is the result when an acid reacts with a metal carbonate?","options":{"A":"Salt, water and carbon dioxide","B":"Filter the acid before mixing","C":"The solution becomes colourless","D":"Metal and carbon"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ACIDMETAL","scaffold":"Acid plus metal carbonate produces a salt, water and carbon dioxide. The gas can be identified because it turns limewater cloudy.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What gas is usually formed when an acid reacts with a metal?","options":{"A":"It neutralises the salt","B":"Carbon dioxide","C":"Hydrogen","D":"Salt, water and carbon dioxide"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-035","stem":"What happens to the position of equilibrium when more reactant is added?","options":{"A":"High temperature always gives the highest yield","B":"It shifts to make more products","C":"It always stops","D":"Temperature has no effect"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-EQUIL","scaffold":"Le Chatelier's principle predicts that equilibrium shifts in the direction that reduces the change. Adding a reactant favours the forward reaction.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What does a catalyst do to a reversible reaction at equilibrium?","options":{"A":"It has no effect on gases","B":"They have different element symbols","C":"Removes one product","D":"Speeds both directions equally"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-041","stem":"why is a burette useful in an acid–alkali titration?","options":{"A":"It measures temperature only","B":"It removes all water","C":"Measured variable volume","D":"It identifies every ion"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-TITRATION","scaffold":"A burette allows a solution to be added accurately while its volume is read. This helps determine the volume needed for neutralisation.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What is the purpose of an indicator in a titration?","options":{"A":"Colour-change end point","B":"It increases the acid concentration","C":"It filters the salt","D":"It prevents neutralisation"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-047","stem":"A reaction in an open flask appears to lose mass. What is the most likely explanation?","options":{"A":"The balance made mass","B":"Atoms were destroyed","C":"The reaction stopped conservation","D":"Escaped gas"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-MASS","scaffold":"If a gas product escapes from an open flask, the measured mass falls. The total mass is still conserved when the escaped gas is included.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Why may a solid product gain mass during combustion?","options":{"A":"Atoms appear from nowhere","B":"Oxygen added","C":"The balance creates oxygen","D":"Combustion destroys mass"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-053","stem":"Which equation links force, mass and acceleration?","options":{"A":"F = m × a","B":"7 N","C":"12 N","D":"F = m + a"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-NEWTON","scaffold":"Newton's second law is F = m × a. The force is measured in newtons when mass is in kilograms and acceleration in m/s².","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A 4 kg trolley accelerates at 3 m/s². What is the resultant force?","options":{"A":"Acceleration increases forever","B":"1.3 N","C":"12 N","D":"0.75 N"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"GCSE-PHASE6-CHEM2-01","stem":"A catalyst increases reaction rate by: ","options":{"A":"increasing the enthalpy change","B":"providing an alternative pathway with lower activation energy","C":"being used up completely","D":"raising the energy of the products and nothing else in the reaction"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-RATE","scaffold":"A catalyst changes the pathway and is regenerated at the end.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies the idea to this case: A catalyst increases reaction rate by: ","options":{"A":"providing an alternative pathway with lower activation energy","B":"a resistor loses all of its stored charge into the rest of the circuit","C":"The activation energy becomes infinitely large for the reaction","D":"By multiplying time by temperature"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"},{"id":"GCSE-PHASE6-CHEM2-02","stem":"A fuel is useful because it releases energy when it: ","options":{"A":"dissolves in water","B":"freezes at room temperature","C":"undergoes combustion","D":"becomes a catalyst"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ENERGETICS","scaffold":"Combustion transfers chemical energy to the surroundings, often as heat.","reforge":{"stem":"In a practical example, which answer fits? A fuel is useful because it releases energy when it: ","options":{"A":"They have the same mass number","B":"undergoes combustion","C":"It always falls","D":"It absorbs heat from the surroundings"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-chem-2"}]},"GCSE-SCI-PHYS-2":{"label":"Physics 2 — Electricity, Magnetism, Particles & Forces in Matter","color":"#2563eb","questions":[{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-01","spec":"1SC0 Topic 1","stem":"What is the unit of potential difference?","options":{"A":"Volt","B":"Ohm","C":"Ampere","D":"Watt"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-UNITS","scaffold":"Potential difference is measured in volts (V). It is the energy transferred per unit charge between two points.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the unit of current?","options":{"A":"The circuit has no current","B":"Volt","C":"Ampere","D":"Ohm"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-02","spec":"1SC0 Topic 10","stem":"A current of 3 A flows through a 4 Ω resistor. What is the potential difference?","options":{"A":"0.75 V","B":"12 V","C":"7 V","D":"1.33 V"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-OHM","scaffold":"Using V = I × R, the potential difference is 3 × 4 = 12 V.","reforge":{"stem":"A 12 V supply drives 3 A through a component. What is its resistance?","options":{"A":"0.25 Ω","B":"36 Ω","C":"9 Ω","D":"4 Ω"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-03","spec":"1SC0 Topic 10","stem":"Why are components connected in parallel in a domestic circuit?","options":{"A":"The circuit has no current","B":"One component controls every branch","C":"Same supply voltage","D":"The cables cannot carry charge"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-HOUSE","scaffold":"In parallel, each branch is connected across the supply. Appliances can operate independently and one branch failing need not break the others.","reforge":{"stem":"What happens if one appliance in a parallel circuit is switched off?","options":{"A":"The other branches can continue","B":"The supply voltage becomes zero","C":"All other branches must stop","D":"Current flows backwards"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-04","spec":"1SC0 Topic 10","stem":"What is the purpose of a fuse in a plug?","options":{"A":"It increases the supply voltage","B":"It makes the appliance more efficient","C":"It stores charge indefinitely","D":"It melts if current becomes too large"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-HOUSE","scaffold":"A fuse contains a wire that melts when excessive current causes heating. This breaks the circuit and helps prevent damage or fire.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the earth wire important in a metal-cased appliance?","options":{"A":"It stores the appliance's energy","B":"Low-resistance fault path","C":"It stops all current in the live wire","D":"It supplies extra power during normal use"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-05","spec":"1SC0 Topic 8","stem":"What does it mean when a force does work on an object?","options":{"A":"Energy transfer","B":"A smaller mass","C":"The force becomes mass","D":"No displacement is possible"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-ENERGY","scaffold":"Work done is the energy transferred when a force causes movement in its direction. W = F × d for a constant force along the displacement.","reforge":{"stem":"A 20 N force moves an object 3 m in its direction. How much work is done?","options":{"A":"6.7 J","B":"23 J","C":"60 J","D":"600 J"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-06","spec":"1SC0 Topic 9","stem":"What happens to the extension of a spring when the force increases within its limit of proportionality?","options":{"A":"It becomes independent of force","B":"Proportional extension","C":"It decreases automatically","D":"Its mass disappears"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-SPRING","scaffold":"Within the limit of proportionality, extension is directly proportional to force. Beyond the elastic limit, the spring may not return to its original length.","reforge":{"stem":"What is elastic deformation?","options":{"A":"The point where the spring has no force","B":"A force disappears","C":"A material gains mass","D":"Returns to original shape"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-07","spec":"1SC0 Topic 12","stem":"What is the motor effect?","options":{"A":"A wire becomes radioactive","B":"A magnet loses all field","C":"Force on current wire","D":"A current stops in a field"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-MOTOR","scaffold":"The motor effect occurs when a current-carrying conductor is placed in a magnetic field. The force direction depends on the field and current directions.","reforge":{"stem":"How can the force on a current-carrying wire be increased?","options":{"A":"More current or field","B":"Remove the magnetic field","C":"Reduce the wire length to zero","D":"Stop the current"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-08","spec":"1SC0 Topic 13","stem":"What is electromagnetic induction?","options":{"A":"A current created by heating a resistor","B":"A charge stored in an insulator","C":"A magnet made from plastic","D":"Induced potential difference"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-TRANSFORM","scaffold":"Moving a magnet relative to a coil changes the magnetic flux and induces a potential difference. A current flows if the circuit is complete.","reforge":{"stem":"How can the induced potential difference be increased?","options":{"A":"Remove the magnetic field","B":"Move the magnet faster","C":"Use fewer coil turns only","D":"Hold the magnet still"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-09","spec":"1SC0 Topic 14","stem":"Why does gas pressure increase when a sealed gas is heated?","options":{"A":"More and harder collisions","B":"Particles stop moving","C":"The gas loses all mass","D":"The container becomes empty"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-GAS","scaffold":"Heating increases the particles' average kinetic energy. They collide with the walls more frequently and with greater force, increasing pressure at constant volume.","reforge":{"stem":"What is absolute zero?","options":{"A":"The pressure of a vacuum","B":"The hottest gas temperature","C":"Lowest temperature","D":"The melting point of iron"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-10","spec":"1SC0 Topic 14","stem":"What is density?","options":{"A":"Weight divided by time","B":"Mass divided by volume","C":"Mass multiplied by force","D":"Volume divided by mass"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-DENSITY","scaffold":"Density is ρ = m ÷ V. A dense material has a large mass for its volume compared with a less dense material.","reforge":{"stem":"A block has mass 600 g and volume 200 cm³. What is its density?","options":{"A":"0.33 g/cm³","B":"120,000 g/cm³","C":"400 g/cm³","D":"3 g/cm³"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-11","spec":"1SC0 Topic 15","stem":"Why does an object float when placed in a liquid?","options":{"A":"Gravity stops acting","B":"Its mass becomes zero","C":"Upthrust balances weight","D":"The liquid has no particles"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-DENSITY","scaffold":"An object floats when the upthrust from the displaced liquid balances its weight. If upthrust is initially greater, the object rises until equilibrium.","reforge":{"stem":"What affects the upthrust on a submerged object?","options":{"A":"Displaced fluid volume","B":"The object's colour","C":"The sound it makes","D":"The number of electrons only"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-12","spec":"1SC0 Topic 15","stem":"What is the relationship between pressure, force and area?","options":{"A":"Pressure = force × area","B":"Pressure = force + area","C":"Pressure = area ÷ force","D":"Pressure = force ÷ area"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-PRESSURE","scaffold":"Pressure is force divided by the area over which it acts. The same force produces greater pressure when applied over a smaller area.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does a sharp knife cut more easily than a blunt one?","options":{"A":"The sharp edge stops friction","B":"Smaller contact area","C":"The blunt edge has no mass","D":"The sharp edge removes gravity"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-13","spec":"1SC0 Topic 8–15","stem":"What happens to the kinetic energy of an object when its speed doubles?","options":{"A":"Four times","B":"It doubles only","C":"Less mass","D":"It halves"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-ENERGY","scaffold":"Kinetic energy is proportional to speed squared: Ek = 1/2 mv². Doubling speed multiplies kinetic energy by four if mass is unchanged.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change increases kinetic energy most for a fixed mass?","options":{"A":"Stopping the object","B":"Halving speed","C":"Doubling speed","D":"Reducing time only"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-14","spec":"1SC0 Topic 8–15","stem":"What is the relationship between electrical power, current and potential difference?","options":{"A":"P = I ÷ V","B":"P = IV","C":"P = V ÷ I","D":"P = I + V"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-ELECTRIC","scaffold":"Electrical power is current multiplied by potential difference. Power is measured in watts when current is in amperes and potential difference in volts.","reforge":{"stem":"A lamp operates at 6 V and 2 A. What is its power?","options":{"A":"0.33 W","B":"3 W","C":"8 W","D":"12 W"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-15","spec":"1SC0 Topic 8–15","stem":"Why does resistance usually increase as a metal wire gets hotter?","options":{"A":"The current becomes light","B":"The wire gains protons","C":"Ion collisions","D":"The length becomes zero"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-RESIST","scaffold":"Heating increases vibrations of the metal ions. Electrons collide more often, making it harder for charge to flow and increasing resistance.","reforge":{"stem":"What happens to the resistance of an LDR when light intensity rises?","options":{"A":"Resistance falls","B":"It becomes infinite","C":"It always doubles","D":"It has no possible change"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-16","spec":"1SC0 Topic 8–15","stem":"What is the current in a series circuit?","options":{"A":"Equal to the resistance","B":"Different in every component","C":"Zero after the first lamp","D":"Same current"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-CIRCUIT","scaffold":"There is only one path in a series circuit, so charge cannot build up at one point. The current is the same through each component.","reforge":{"stem":"What happens to total resistance when another resistor is added in series?","options":{"A":"It becomes zero","B":"Resistance increases","C":"It is always unchanged","D":"It becomes a current"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-17","spec":"1SC0 Topic 8–15","stem":"Why can a thermistor be used in a temperature-sensing circuit?","options":{"A":"Variable resistance","B":"It produces no current","C":"It is unaffected by heat","D":"It stores nuclear energy"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-RESIST","scaffold":"A thermistor's resistance varies with temperature. In a potential-divider circuit this can produce a voltage that changes with temperature.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one use of a thermistor?","options":{"A":"Generating alpha particles","B":"Measuring mass directly","C":"Temperature control","D":"Replacing every fuse"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-18","spec":"1SC0 Topic 8–15","stem":"What determines the direction of the force in the motor effect?","options":{"A":"The colour of the wire","B":"Current and field directions","C":"The mass of the battery only","D":"A wire becomes radioactive"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-MOTOR","scaffold":"Fleming's left-hand rule links the directions of magnetic field, conventional current and force. Reversing the current or field reverses the force.","reforge":{"stem":"How can a motor's turning effect be increased?","options":{"A":"Use a shorter coil with no turns","B":"Remove the magnet","C":"Stop the current","D":"More current or field"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-19","spec":"1SC0 Topic 8–15","stem":"Why does a transformer need an alternating current?","options":{"A":"It removes all energy transfer","B":"Direct current changes direction constantly","C":"Changing magnetic field","D":"It stops induction"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-TRANSFORM","scaffold":"An alternating current produces a changing magnetic field in the primary coil. This changing field induces a potential difference in the secondary coil.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a step-up transformer do?","options":{"A":"Higher potential difference","B":"Increases charge stored forever","C":"Removes the magnetic field","D":"Changes AC into mass"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-20","spec":"1SC0 Topic 8–15","stem":"Why is electricity transmitted at high potential difference?","options":{"A":"It stops energy transfer","B":"It increases heating losses","C":"It makes resistance zero","D":"Lower current"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-ELECTRIC","scaffold":"For a given power, a higher potential difference means lower current. Cable heating losses depend on I²R, so reducing current reduces wasted energy.","reforge":{"stem":"What causes heating in transmission cables?","options":{"A":"No charge movement","B":"Cable resistance heating","C":"A lack of electrons in the source","D":"The absence of a magnetic field"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-21","spec":"1SC0 Topic 8–15","stem":"What happens to gas pressure if its volume decreases at constant temperature?","options":{"A":"Pressure increases","B":"Pressure becomes zero","C":"Pressure always falls","D":"Particles stop colliding"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-GAS","scaffold":"Reducing volume gives particles less space. They collide with the container walls more frequently, increasing pressure if temperature is constant.","reforge":{"stem":"What must remain constant when investigating pressure and volume?","options":{"A":"The label on the container","B":"The colour of the gas","C":"Constant temperature","D":"The number of graphs"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-22","spec":"1SC0 Topic 8–15","stem":"Why can a metal sink while a less massive object floats?","options":{"A":"Mass alone determines floating","B":"Mass and volume","C":"Gravity acts only on metals","D":"Volume has no effect"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-DENSITY","scaffold":"Floating depends on density and the upthrust compared with weight. A large-volume object can have lower average density than a smaller, more massive object.","reforge":{"stem":"What happens to density if mass stays constant but volume increases?","options":{"A":"It remains a force","B":"It increases","C":"It becomes infinite","D":"Lower density"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-23","spec":"1SC0 Topic 8–15","stem":"Why does a liquid exert greater pressure at greater depth?","options":{"A":"Gravity stops acting near the surface","B":"The liquid has fewer particles","C":"More liquid weight","D":"Area becomes zero"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-LIQUID","scaffold":"Liquid pressure increases with depth because the column of liquid above exerts a greater weight per unit area. Pressure also depends on density and gravitational field strength.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change increases pressure at the same depth in a liquid?","options":{"A":"Denser liquid","B":"Using a less dense liquid","C":"Reducing gravity to zero","D":"Removing the liquid"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"SCI-PHYS2-24","spec":"1SC0 Topic 8–15","stem":"What is the limit of proportionality for a spring?","options":{"A":"The length of the ruler","B":"The point where the spring has no force","C":"The mass of the spring","D":"Beyond proportional extension"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-SPRING","scaffold":"Up to the limit of proportionality, extension is proportional to force. Beyond it, Hooke's law no longer applies even if the spring has not yet broken.","reforge":{"stem":"What may happen if a spring is stretched beyond its elastic limit?","options":{"A":"It becomes perfectly elastic","B":"Permanent extension","C":"Its mass disappears","D":"Its resistance becomes zero"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-006","stem":"Two heterozygous parents have a child. What is the probability that the child inherits two recessive alleles?","options":{"A":"25%","B":"0%","C":"Each cell receives different genes","D":"75%"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-GENETICS","scaffold":"A cross of two heterozygous parents gives four equally likely allele combinations. One combination contains two recessive alleles, so the probability is 1/4 or 25%.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"For two heterozygous parents, what is the probability of a dominant phenotype when the dominant allele is fully expressed?","options":{"A":"25%","B":"Two identical dominant alleles","C":"75%","D":"100%"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-012","stem":"Why can monoclonal antibodies be useful in medicine?","options":{"A":"They increase all hormone levels","B":"They bind to one specific target","C":"They work only on healthy cells","D":"They replace every enzyme"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-MONOCLONAL","scaffold":"Monoclonal antibodies are identical antibodies made from a single clone of cells. Their specificity allows them to bind to a particular antigen or cell marker.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What feature makes a monoclonal antibody specific?","options":{"A":"It has no protein structure","B":"It changes into a red blood cell","C":"It destroys every nearby cell","D":"Specific antigen binding"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-018","stem":"What is the purpose of a vector in genetic modification?","options":{"A":"It destroys every chromosome","B":"It prevents DNA replication","C":"Carries a desired gene","D":"It removes all variation"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-VECTOR","scaffold":"A vector, such as a plasmid or virus, can carry a desired gene into a target cell. The gene may then be expressed by that cell.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which enzyme is used to join a desired gene into a vector?","options":{"A":"Ligase","B":"Amylase","C":"Lipase","D":"Catalase"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-024","stem":"How can a rapid environmental change cause a species to become extinct?","options":{"A":"All competitors disappear","B":"Every individual gains a useful mutation","C":"The environment stops selecting","D":"Adaptation cannot keep up"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-BIO-EVOLUTION","scaffold":"If conditions change faster than a population can adapt, survival and reproduction may fall. The population can decline to extinction.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which factor can increase extinction risk?","options":{"A":"Greater genetic variation","B":"Habitat loss","C":"A stable food supply","D":"Successful conservation"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-030","stem":"Why does a strong acid have a lower pH than a weak acid of the same concentration?","options":{"A":"It ionises more completely","B":"It has no hydrogen ions","C":"Ten times more hydrogen ions","D":"It is always more concentrated"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ACID","scaffold":"A strong acid ionises almost completely in water, producing a higher concentration of hydrogen ions and therefore a lower pH.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What does a pH decrease from 4 to 3 represent?","options":{"A":"A neutral solution","B":"One tenth as many hydrogen ions","C":"Ten times more hydrogen ions","D":"No change in acidity"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-036","stem":"Why may industry use a compromise temperature for an exothermic equilibrium reaction?","options":{"A":"It removes the catalyst","B":"Faster rate, lower yield","C":"Temperature has no effect","D":"Low temperature gives no products"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-EQUIL","scaffold":"For an exothermic forward reaction, lower temperature favours products but slows the rate. Industry chooses a compromise between yield and production speed.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What is the effect of increasing pressure on an equilibrium with fewer gas molecules on the product side?","options":{"A":"It always stops","B":"It destroys the catalyst","C":"Faster rate, lower yield","D":"Fewer gas molecules"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-042","stem":"what happens to positive ions at the cathode during electrolysis?","options":{"A":"They lose neutrons","B":"They form neutral gases only","C":"Gain electrons","D":"They move through the wire"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-ELECTRO","scaffold":"Positive ions are attracted to the negative cathode. They gain electrons in reduction reactions at the electrode.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What happens to negative ions at the anode?","options":{"A":"Lose electrons","B":"They gain protons","C":"They become the power supply","D":"They stop moving"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-048","stem":"What does a single spot on a chromatogram usually suggest about a sample?","options":{"A":"It is necessarily radioactive","B":"It contains every possible dye","C":"It has no solvent","D":"One soluble substance"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SEP-CHEM-CHROM","scaffold":"A single spot can indicate one soluble component under the conditions used. Comparing positions with known substances can support identification.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What does the distance travelled by a spot help compare?","options":{"A":"The mass of the paper","B":"Compare Rf values","C":"The room temperature only","D":"The number of protons"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"GCSE-SCIENCE-COV-054","stem":"How is energy conserved?","options":{"A":"Energy is transferred, not created or destroyed","B":"Energy is destroyed by friction between the surfaces","C":"Only useful energy is conserved","D":"Useful force multiplied by distance only"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-ENERGY","scaffold":"The total energy of a closed system remains constant. Transfers may spread energy to the surroundings, often by heating.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"What happens to energy dissipated by friction?","options":{"A":"It disappears","B":"Halving speed","C":"Thermal energy","D":"It becomes mass"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"GCSE-PHASE6-PHYS2-01","stem":"The resistance of a component is calculated using: ","options":{"A":"Trees create carbon dioxide by absorbing light","B":"potential difference divided by current","C":"power divided by time","D":"0.75 V"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-OHM","scaffold":"Ohm's law gives resistance R = V ÷ I.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies the idea to this case: The resistance of a component is calculated using: ","options":{"A":"potential difference divided by current","B":"The allele appears because the population tries","C":"1.33 V","D":"36 Ω"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"},{"id":"GCSE-PHASE6-PHYS2-02","stem":"An electric motor uses the motor effect when: ","options":{"A":"a resistor loses all of its stored charge into the rest of the circuit","B":"a transformer has no coils","C":"a current-carrying conductor experiences a force in a magnetic field","D":"a fuse increases current"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SEP-PHYS-MOTOR","scaffold":"The force on a current-carrying conductor can produce rotation.","reforge":{"stem":"In a practical example, which answer fits? An electric motor uses the motor effect when: ","options":{"A":"Use a much shorter coil that has no turns of wire wound around the core","B":"a current-carrying conductor experiences a force in a magnetic field","C":"Ampere","D":"Reduce the wire length to zero"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-science-phys-2"}]}});
